WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.454
- Thank you and welcome
everybody to the public board

00:00:06.454 --> 00:00:09.120
of NHS England.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.550
The session, again, will be live streamed

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.847
and we have members of the public

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:18.300
invited to observe the meeting.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.066
So welcome everybody.

00:00:20.066 --> 00:00:22.950
We do have some apologies for absence.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.080
So we've got apologies from Rakesh Kapoor,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.112
who's abroad, from Susan Kilsby,

00:00:28.112 --> 00:00:29.790
who did join us this morning
at the private board,

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.710
but dialled in from overseas

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.603
and from Mark Walport, who
actually is indisposed.

00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:40.440
Vin Diwakar is away,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.500
but John Quinn is here,
deputising for Vin.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.710
And Steve Powis is away,
but Aidan Fowler is here

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.693
deputising for Steve.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.240
I would just like to
comment that we are losing

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.149
three of our non-executive directors

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:59.220
that this is their last meeting.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.050
And I'd just like to take the opportunity

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.080
on behalf of the board
and many, many colleagues

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.712
across NHS England just to acknowledge

00:01:07.712 --> 00:01:12.330
their contribution and to
thank them for all their work.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.330
So Rakesh Kapoor is stepping
down at the end of this year

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.765
and he's been a very strong
non-executive person,

00:01:20.765 --> 00:01:24.293
in particular, not just at the board,

00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:25.380
but also sitting on the
audit and risk committee

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.370
and also on on DDAT, Susan Kilsby,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.850
also stepping down at
the end of this year.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.250
Also a member of the
audit and risk committee

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.280
and also the people and
remuneration committee.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.880
and lastly, Munir, who's in the room here.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.217
Munir, we will miss you hugely

00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:46.790
and thank you for your
excellent leadership actually

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:47.910
of the quality committee

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.520
as well as the constant contribution

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.900
you've made to this board

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.690
and the work of the NHS.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.470
Separately, we are, as the
board knows, hoping to have

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.460
approval for new non-executive
directors to join the board.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.230
We did interview candidates
in the first half of July,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.450
and we're hoping we'll get approval

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.450
and sign off certainly before Christmas.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.080
Can I then just ask are
there any new declarations

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.890
of interest from any members
of the board since we last met?

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.813
- There'll be mine added to
the formal documents, Richard.

00:02:23.813 --> 00:02:25.410
- They are, yep, thank you Emily.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.730
And actually that's a very good prompt

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.250
just to welcome you Emily.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.600
So Emily Lawson is our interim
chief operating officer, COO.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.520
And it's a real pleasure
to have you as part

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.130
of the team and we're delighted.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.363
- [Emily] Thank you.

00:02:39.363 --> 00:02:42.630
- So minutes from the last

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.603
board meeting held on the 5th of October.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.350
Is the board happy if I
adopt those, sign those?

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.970
Thank you very much, let me move to

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.070
sort of a broader chairs update.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.400
I mean we have a helpful agenda,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.110
an interesting agenda this afternoon.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.290
A couple of things that we
discussed at the private board

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.780
this morning, not on
this afternoon's agenda,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.292
but we're also very good discussions.

00:03:09.292 --> 00:03:12.900
One of those is actually
reflecting on medium term strategy

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.750
and direction and how that translates

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.360
across into planning guidance.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.610
And separately actually
a very good conversation,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.790
which is a continuing theme
where Julian and Amanda

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.850
and the team overall has
an increasingly good grip,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.603
which is on productivity
and on efficiency.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.850
The board has met several times

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.860
since the last sort of formal board,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.870
so what we call deep dives.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.213
And we've had a number of those.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.562
We certainly had a very good session

00:03:45.562 --> 00:03:49.200
just less than a week
ago on winter planning,

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.500
just updating where we are

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:52.980
as we look to winter.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.160
We had two sessions actually
on, on financial outlook

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.000
and financial performance led by Julian

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.170
as we looked at the
consequences of how we manage

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.549
with government, the cost of
industrial action this year.

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:10.170
And clearly industrial action will feature

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.150
later on in this conversation.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.880
And then we had a very
good strategic conversation

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.950
about risk appetite

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.590
and that was an important conversation

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.433
and I think we reached some
very thoughtful conclusions.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.533
What are we seeing?

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:27.963
We're seeing continuing lift,

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.080
significant lift in demand actually,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.980
and also therefore the scale
of provision by the NHS.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.090
So just some of those demand
levels stats that I carry

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.577
around that if I look at primary care

00:04:41.577 --> 00:04:43.140
and the truly excellent job actually

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.090
that are our general practitioners

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.480
and the workforce in primary care do,

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.480
they are providing 17% more
appointments than in 2019.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.097
And we're seeing more than 10%
of the population of England.

00:05:02.097 --> 00:05:05.100
It's about 12% are having
an appointment every week.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.900
So 12% of the population

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.490
has a primary care appointment every week.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:13.200
And of those almost half as we
know are provided on the day

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.600
that the call requesting
an appointment is made.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.538
And it's over 70% of those appointments

00:05:17.538 --> 00:05:22.134
are physical face-to-face
as opposed to virtual.

00:05:22.134 --> 00:05:25.680
In cancer checks, if I think about that,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.330
referrals for cancer checks,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.975
we're doing almost 30% more
than we were doing again,

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:33.660
pre the pandemic.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.110
This year, and even more
recently in October,

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.350
again, we're seeing
significantly lifting demand.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.856
Amanda may well talk about it,

00:05:42.856 --> 00:05:44.850
but in October, I think we
saw an 8% lift in admissions

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.850
from the October of the prior year.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:52.570
And we had record emergency
department attendance in October

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:57.180
with I think 2.2 million
people attending A&amp;E

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.460
or ED in October.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.200
And our ambulance cat one

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.034
and cat two call volumes,

00:06:03.034 --> 00:06:05.768
call receipts were again
the highest we've seen.

00:06:05.768 --> 00:06:08.860
So the NHS is seeing
very significant demand,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:13.860
but is also providing a scale
of provision to meet that.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.603
Now we're getting into winter,
clearly we're in winter

00:06:16.603 --> 00:06:20.550
and one of the meetings
I referred to earlier

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.240
that the board had actually
was with Sarah Jane

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.260
and Emily just looking
at winter preparations.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:31.260
And there I think NHS
stands in much better state

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.830
than it was this time last
year in its preparation.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.686
But clearly winter I think

00:06:36.686 --> 00:06:39.750
will always be very challenging for us,

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.820
particularly around I think bed capacity.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.150
But one stat I would always
share is this time last year

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.680
we were looking at a target
of virtual ward build out

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.910
and we've set ourselves a
target of 10,000 virtual wards,

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.830
I think by end of September.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.397
And that felt very ambitious,
but actually we hit that

00:06:59.397 --> 00:07:03.660
and I think we're now at
about 10,700 virtual wards

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.550
and continuing to develop those,
those further and clearly.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.689
We've also added CDCs, I think 40 CDCs

00:07:10.689 --> 00:07:13.710
have been added in the
course of this year.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.150
And the other stat I tend to carry

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.300
in my head is if we look
at same day emergency care,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.450
those now are operating seven days a week,

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:24.630
12 hours a day, and 88% of our trust.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.790
So actually if I think about our capacity

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.790
and our, our ability to
hit, to meet the demand

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.227
as it comes at us in winter,

00:07:31.227 --> 00:07:34.050
and I haven't talked about
vaccination, well actually again,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:35.340
we started earlier

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:39.270
and I think we've done 11
million COVID vaccinations

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.280
and 16 million flu vaccinations,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.420
which are the highest we've ever achieved

00:07:44.572 --> 00:07:45.405
before getting into winter.

00:07:45.405 --> 00:07:49.350
So there's lots of extremely
good stuff achieved.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.373
Now industrial action,
I have to comment on,

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:59.570
but I would defer more I think to Amanda,

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.942
but I, industrial action,
we've already essentially

00:08:03.942 --> 00:08:08.942
dealt with 40 days I
think, of industrial action

00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:13.680
across the NHS this year.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.030
And I would say that the days chosen now

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.590
by the junior doctors,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.990
they could not be at a worse time.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.120
So in, in, in the run up before Christmas

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.640
and in that first week of
January, that's a really,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.985
really difficult time
for the NHS to manage.

00:08:29.985 --> 00:08:33.780
I know actually that
the NHS will rise to it

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.480
and will work hugely hard
to meet the consequences

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:37.950
of that disruption,

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.440
but actually it will put the NHS

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.960
under very real pressure.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.370
But I know Amanda will say more

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.830
financial pressures are
tough at the moment.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.020
Julian will give us that update

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.360
and particularly also thinking
about financial pressures

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.766
then running into next year.

00:08:56.766 --> 00:08:59.160
And those financial pressures also come

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.680
as much from inflation,

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.090
which we're seeing embedding many of us,

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:06.150
we see embedding across
society more broadly

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:11.150
and we will clearly also suffer those.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.233
Now I would just like to spend
a couple minutes, if I might,

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:18.820
just thanking the board

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.025
and within the board clearly
thanking the executives.

00:09:24.025 --> 00:09:26.430
It's the board in December.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.730
I know we worked to a
sort of a financial year

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:30.930
rather than a calendar year,

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.357
but as one gets into the Christmas month,

00:09:33.357 --> 00:09:37.473
I always feel it's quite
helpful to reflect back a bit.

00:09:38.332 --> 00:09:40.410
And I think there's been huge achievement

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:44.850
for which I hugely thank the executives

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:48.270
and all the people actually
working across the NHS.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:50.370
So the long-term workforce plan,

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.890
we're going to hear a bit
about it again this afternoon.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.067
It's a major step forward for the NHS

00:09:56.067 --> 00:09:59.277
and Navina under your guidance

00:09:59.277 --> 00:10:01.230
and leadership working with the government

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:02.063
and with the support of the government.

00:10:02.907 --> 00:10:04.650
We're now in, in execution mode,

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:06.000
the federated data platform.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.079
We'll hear about that,
mean hugely well done.

00:10:08.079 --> 00:10:11.370
I think it's a major step forward

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:14.700
for the NHS in terms of
actually synthesising

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:16.380
data across our secondary systems

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.720
and allowing us to get
process improvement.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.900
And if everyone wants to
think about how to enable AI

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.250
to bring its benefit,

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.530
actually having a federated data platform,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.290
connecting that layer of data, I think,

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.840
is a very powerful example of it.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:35.010
Electronic patient records
now in 90% of trusts,

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:39.720
the NHS app now has 33
million subscribers.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.660
We do just under three
million repeat prescriptions

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.283
every month via the app.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.100
It didn't exist seven or eight years ago,

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.018
six or seven years ago.

00:10:49.018 --> 00:10:51.468
It's transformational in
terms of how the NHS interacts

00:10:51.468 --> 00:10:53.520
with its patients.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.890
And in October we did
just over three million,

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.850
3.2 million appointments
on the app into secondary.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.920
So actually it's beginning
to cross fertilise across

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.920
between primary and secondary

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.280
via a system which actually
people are becoming

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.270
increasingly comfortable in using.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:16.770
More narrowly to us, again,
huge thanks to the executives

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.080
and I'd like to thank all the workforce

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.020
of the NHS England as merged.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:25.064
So digital and HEE and X,

00:11:25.064 --> 00:11:30.064
we've merged five entities, four boards

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.820
and have reduced headcounts.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.660
So taken 35% of the headcount out.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.610
That's put huge stress
on the organisation.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:42.053
I have a lot of sympathy for
people who've been affected

00:11:42.053 --> 00:11:43.350
by it, but actually it's a very important

00:11:44.272 --> 00:11:46.170
bureaucratization of the centre

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.500
and it enables the ICBs effectually for us

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.920
to devolve more down to the ICBs

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.930
actually within the regions,
the regions have actually

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.450
taken out 40% of headcounts.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:00.450
It's a very important debureaucratization

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:04.260
of the center's engagement
with the systems.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.674
But the centre is smaller

00:12:06.674 --> 00:12:07.507
but still expert.

00:12:07.507 --> 00:12:11.580
Electives, I think we
had through the year,

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.140
Amanda made, again,

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.720
under your leadership and with Jim Mackey

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.530
and others, again, we made significant

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.540
steps forward in this period,

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.970
industrial action clearly
broke the trajectory

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.649
of improvement that we were seeing.

00:12:26.649 --> 00:12:29.785
and I would hope very much
that we'll see a trajectory

00:12:29.785 --> 00:12:34.785
reestablished once industrial
action hopefully is sorted.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:40.110
And then lastly, as a theme,

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:43.020
and I increasingly talk
about this when I go

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.740
outside the NHS, we sometimes
lose sight of just the level

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.740
of innovation that the
NHS sees and implements.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.560
And that innovation is utterly remarkable.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.230
It's truly remarkable.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.970
And there's a phrase
you've heard me use before

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.160
that Amanda taught me, actually,

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.069
it's a very simple phrase.

00:13:04.069 --> 00:13:05.850
It was easy to teach, which is the NHS

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:08.430
is no longer the NHS of the iron lung.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:10.200
And if you think about the NHS today

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.740
and think about what it was
three years ago or six years ago

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:18.030
or 12 years ago, it is
transformed in terms of its level

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.490
of innovation and the level of treatment

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.517
and care that it provides
to the population.

00:13:23.517 --> 00:13:25.620
and and where are the examples,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.393
the more recent examples of that,

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.240
and my pronunciation
is always pretty poor,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.436
but we had two, three
weeks ago anastrozole,

00:13:33.436 --> 00:13:34.860
so a repurposed drug.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:36.510
It cost four pence per a day.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.550
Extremely effective in the treatment

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.700
breast cancer. Semaglutide,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.118
which is the anti-obesity drug.

00:13:44.118 --> 00:13:49.118
We're very close to a cancer
vaccine platform in genomics

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.710
where we are world leading
in whole genome sequencing.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:55.440
But actually as importantly,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.440
in single and panel testing
last year we did 700,000 tests

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.750
and we're scanning, 700,000
patients were tested

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.640
and we're scanning for 200 cancers

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.710
and 3,200 rare diseases.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.718
And then we have other
programmes as we know,

00:14:11.718 --> 00:14:15.115
like Grail Galleri, which
is 140,000 population

00:14:15.115 --> 00:14:19.830
in that testing, that experiment.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:23.719
And that looks to identify over 50 cancers

00:14:23.719 --> 00:14:27.060
from a blood test.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:31.140
And other than that we
have brilliant technology

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.240
evolution, evolution.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.010
I know our technology and the systems

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:38.337
and our data is often very
difficult down in the trust

00:14:38.337 --> 00:14:41.310
and the legacies in that we
have to focus on improving.

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:43.570
But my comments around
the NHS app, around FDP,

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.410
I think they're an important step forward

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.320
in the way we're thinking
about technology.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.543
And we also do now have teledermatology

00:14:51.543 --> 00:14:54.360
in terms of spotting cancers.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.120
We are increasingly
using AI in our imaging.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.450
So actually there are real
signs of progress here

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.916
in terms of innovation within the NHS

00:15:03.916 --> 00:15:05.580
and I think it's quite easy sometimes,

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:07.372
and I'm not going to get Pollyannish

00:15:07.372 --> 00:15:11.880
because our world is extremely
pressured, demands are high,

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.880
our ability to meet the patient's needs.

00:15:15.735 --> 00:15:17.198
We work really hard to do that.

00:15:17.198 --> 00:15:18.502
There's lots of stresses in the system.

00:15:18.502 --> 00:15:19.814
So I'm not being Pollyannish,

00:15:19.814 --> 00:15:21.120
but we also must not lose sight, I think.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.100
What I believe has been a whole series

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:25.200
of achievements and
particularly in the last year

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:26.790
and two years and three years

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.500
and that's actually coming through

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:29.850
a period of a pandemic.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:33.570
So I circled back to
my thanks to the board

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:35.670
but in particular my
thanks to the executives

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.670
and then all of the other
managers around the system

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.470
as well as to our clinicians and nurses

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:42.600
and so on who are providing
this level of care

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:44.940
to the population.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:49.320
So I think I have
probably finished on that

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.080
and I'm going to hand over Amanda to you.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.660
- Great, thank you very
much indeed Richard

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:56.400
and I'll touch on some of the things

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.930
you've queued me up for,

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.220
but just to start by saying that of course

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:00.390
since our last board meeting

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.580
we have seen a government reshuffle.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:04.860
So I've had the pleasure of
meeting our new secretary

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:06.900
of state Victoria Atkins

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:09.210
and I think we all look forward to working

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.476
with the new ministerial
team as well as some

00:16:12.476 --> 00:16:14.408
of the ministers who've
stayed on in the department

00:16:14.408 --> 00:16:17.550
and maintaining a really
constructive relationship

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:19.874
between the NHS and government.

00:16:19.874 --> 00:16:23.130
So the first thing I wanted
to say, I wanted to add,

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:26.010
if I may, Richard to your
welcome, a particular welcome

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.610
to Emily who of course was
can we say you were on loan

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.153
- [Emily] That is actually what I was.

00:16:32.792 --> 00:16:33.960
- I think you were on loan to head up

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:36.900
the number 10 delivery unit

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.504
and now returns to us with a huge amount

00:16:39.504 --> 00:16:41.040
of delivery expertise from
government as well of course

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.840
as having led to the
vaccination programme,

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:46.410
the COVID vaccination programme
here incredibly successfully

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:48.390
and much experience before that.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.373
So Emily, it's great to have you back.

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.490
I did want to say if I may Richard,

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.444
a bit about industrial action.

00:16:55.444 --> 00:16:56.280
I know that this is something
that other colleagues

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.973
will then pick up when
they're giving their updates.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.030
The industrial action announced this week

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:05.130
by the BMA, I think is top

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.890
of everyone's minds and worry lists.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.020
And that is true of us as a board

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.940
but also right across the NHS
since we last met as a board,

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:18.360
there haven't been any strikes
and that's because ministers

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:20.100
and the trade unions
have been in discussions

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:22.200
about reaching a pay settlement.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.690
And it is worth noting
to your point Richard,

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.667
that since the last set

00:17:26.667 --> 00:17:28.740
of strikes we've seen
improvement every single week

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:32.430
in reducing long waits for patients.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.580
So it is very positive that
the government announced

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:38.100
that they and the unions
representing consultant doctors

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:39.510
in England have reached a position

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:42.750
whereby the unions are
able to put an offer

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:46.170
to members following
constructive negotiations.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:50.790
However, junior doctors have
this week announced new rounds

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:52.080
of action and that news

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.491
is obviously incredibly disappointing

00:17:55.491 --> 00:17:58.440
for everybody involved,
particularly because it comes

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.650
at an already very
challenging time for the NHS.

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:05.073
We have seen them now
in our weekly sit reps,

00:18:06.161 --> 00:18:07.823
which are starting to
be reported publicly.

00:18:08.677 --> 00:18:10.530
We can see escalating winter
pressures with flu, COVID,

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:13.050
norovirus, for norovirus in particular,

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.510
actually just worth noting
we are at a worse point

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:17.130
than we were last year.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:21.510
And that is on top of just
huge demand across the board.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:26.510
So over previous periods
of industrial action.

00:18:26.748 --> 00:18:31.748
Richard, you've mentioned
some of this already,

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:35.430
but we have seen not
just a significant impact

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.290
on patients waiting for elective care

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.720
but also increased risk to patients

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.690
who need time critical care too.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:46.290
And we mustn't forget, I know
nobody works in NHS ever does,

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.320
but just forget that behind
every postponed appointment

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:53.310
or procedure is a patient
experiencing added distress,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.620
anxiety and pain.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:58.830
So this will be further
heightened at this incredibly busy

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:01.952
time of year, as we've discussed

00:19:01.952 --> 00:19:04.385
before as a board, pay as
a matter for government.

00:19:04.385 --> 00:19:07.890
And this dispute is between
the government and trade unions

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.310
and NHS England's role continues to focus

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:13.454
on doing everything we possibly can

00:19:13.454 --> 00:19:16.740
to keep patients as safe as possible

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:19.050
and minimise disruption where we can.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:22.350
So we will be working with the wider NHS

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:24.870
and engaging with unions to do that.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:26.880
If these strikes go ahead.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.730
Overall though, it's worth
just saying we will have seen

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.170
then a full year

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:34.590
of strike action if the
strikes in December go ahead.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.310
So that would mean that across 2023,

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:42.090
we'd have had almost 800
hours of industrial action

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:46.950
by hospital doctors in
total back to 792 hours.

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:51.930
And just if you think about
that roughly, so that's 9%.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.153
So that's almost a 10th of a full year.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.930
So our position remains that we respect

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.060
the right of staff to
take industrial action

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.790
and the decisions of
those who choose not to.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.550
We value our staff, we
absolutely understand

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.230
that good paying conditions are important

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.390
not only for individuals
and their families

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:11.370
but for wider issues such as retention

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:15.510
and recruitment, which
impact on patient care too.

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:16.950
But while many people are preparing

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:20.090
for Christmas celebrations
here in the NHS,

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:24.630
we have a huge job ahead of
us preparing for strikes.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:28.290
So I know Emily and Aidan will
talk more about that shortly.

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:30.840
But just to reiterate, our immediate focus

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:34.860
is to support the NHS to
plan, to respond to the action

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.890
and also to discuss with the
BMA, how to reduce the risk

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:41.280
of harm to patient. Over the coming weeks,

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:44.700
I just ask all colleagues
to bear this in mind,

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.130
We will be making clear to our patients

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:49.140
and the public that they must continue

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:51.750
to come forward when
they need urgent care.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:53.220
So it's a really important message

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.220
that we need to continue
to give consistently

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:58.980
and we will make sure, we must
make sure that we take time

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.010
to thank staff who will be
covering for their colleagues

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:03.330
and doing their best

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:06.213
to provide the best
patient care that they can.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.968
I just finished on this
section if I may, Richard,

00:21:09.968 --> 00:21:11.271
by just saying, you know,

00:21:11.271 --> 00:21:14.010
we absolutely cannot have
a winter like last year.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:15.930
That was the worst of my own career

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:20.040
and clearly strikes will
make this even harder.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.920
So I don't think anyone here
at NHS England would disagree

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.560
that what we would like to
see of course is a resolution

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.630
to the dispute as soon as possible

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:30.360
to prevent any further
impact on patient care,

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:32.370
which let's be clear

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.430
is an inevitable consequence
of strike action.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:38.370
So we would urge all sides
to get back round the table

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.533
to continue negotiations.

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:43.996
I'll let others pick up on that

00:21:43.996 --> 00:21:45.923
'cause I know that will be
a theme Richard, as we talk.

00:21:45.923 --> 00:21:47.550
If I just move on though

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.770
for a moment just to talk
about the finance and planning

00:21:51.715 --> 00:21:53.250
because obviously
financially, industrial action

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.250
has had a big impact this year.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.740
We do now have clarity since we last met

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:00.480
about how the government want us to deal

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:03.120
with those costs from the strikes so far.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:06.420
So we have been able to release
now hundreds of millions

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:09.050
to give straight to systems

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.430
so that they can prioritise patient safety

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:14.370
and urgent emergency
care going into winter.

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:17.310
Even so, Julian might want
to say a bit more about this

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:20.577
later, but we do know difficult
choices have had to be made

00:22:20.577 --> 00:22:22.650
and will have to be made nationally

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:25.950
and locally to balance the
books in this financial year.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:28.832
We are of course also having conversations

00:22:28.832 --> 00:22:31.440
about the next financial
year now with government

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:35.040
and with systems to establish
what credible delivery plans

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.773
would look like within
available resources.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.620
A few more things I wanted
to touch on, if I may.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.960
We're going to get deep, I know shortly

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:45.000
into a discussion about winter

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.050
because if we think about

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.150
where the operational focus is right now,

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:51.457
maintaining services and
patient safety through winter,

00:22:51.457 --> 00:22:53.010
is obviously top of the list.

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:54.060
It's not the only thing on the list

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:56.430
but it is top of the list.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:23:00.060
And as a reminder, we
started preparing for winter

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.920
much earlier than we ever have before.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.540
Richard, you've called
out some of the things

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:06.330
that we've done, things
like the virtual ward beds,

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:10.080
which are obviously really
important pieces of that jigsaw.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.650
A key focus has been on
ambulance response times

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:16.140
and I know Emily will say more about this,

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.750
but it is worth, as you said, just noting

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:20.730
that whilst there's still more to do

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:24.224
and we're absolutely still determined

00:23:24.224 --> 00:23:27.770
to support the NHS to do
the best it possibly can.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:31.380
If we look at the last
monthly figures in October,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:34.530
category two responses were
almost 20 minutes faster

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:35.940
than this time last year.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.597
And that was despite that additional 8%

00:23:38.597 --> 00:23:40.890
higher level of admissions.

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:43.440
So we continue to deliver our winter plans

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:46.920
and I'm continuing to be really impressed

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:48.100
with the determination

00:23:48.945 --> 00:23:50.520
and engagement of
colleagues across the NHS

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:52.200
in trying to do their absolute best

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.109
to be as ready as possible

00:23:54.109 --> 00:23:57.300
to look after patients over this winter.

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:58.620
At the same time, we are continuing

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.290
to deliver our winter
COVID and flu campaign.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:04.860
So Steve and team have
done an excellent job

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:09.780
on particularly rolling out
the COVID booster campaign

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:11.040
and the flu campaign.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.140
So we're now at, Steve will
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:15.840
over 11.4 million COVID boosters

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:18.270
and 16.6 million flu vaccines.

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:19.800
And there's still a bit of time left

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:22.080
to go in our public campaign.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.820
That's important 'cause it's about

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.770
of course enabling
patients to take the steps

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:28.140
that they can take to
look after themselves

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:30.870
and minimise their own personal risk.

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:34.290
It is fair to say though, I
think that we are concerned

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:38.820
that uptake rates in frontline health

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:43.309
and care workers are in some places lower,

00:24:43.309 --> 00:24:46.020
certainly lower than they were last year

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:47.670
and lower than we would like.

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:50.190
So I know that regional
teams have been working

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:53.788
with those trusts with the lowest rates

00:24:53.788 --> 00:24:56.130
to understand what further
support they might need.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:57.720
Again, that might be something Ruth

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:01.260
and Aidan want to talk about
when they come in later

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:04.954
'cause clearly supporting
our staff to stay well

00:25:04.954 --> 00:25:08.160
and healthy over winter
is a huge priority for us.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:11.160
You've talked about the FDP.
I just wanted to add my thanks

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:12.610
to Vin, to Jackie, to Julie

00:25:13.663 --> 00:25:15.574
and Chris, Emily, all
colleagues who've helped get

00:25:15.574 --> 00:25:18.180
the procurement for the FDP over the line.

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:20.610
I do think when we look back
at this year, we will see it

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:23.010
as one of the most significant investments

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.603
that we will have made
in the future of the NHS

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:28.140
and a really big moment.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:30.030
But we have been really
conscious of the need

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.560
to take people with us on this.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:33.300
So there's been an enormous amount of work

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.370
to engage colleagues and stakeholders

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:38.490
and to ensure a incredibly
thorough procurement

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.950
process over many months.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:45.870
So the real work we know
starts here and I know that Vin

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:48.990
and others will be keeping
the board up to date

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:52.230
as they will be and as Ming
will explain later, continuing

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:56.799
to engage with ICBs
with trusts to make sure

00:25:56.799 --> 00:25:59.010
that we are really making
the maximum benefit

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.190
from this procurement.

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.220
Final word you've said
it already, Richard,

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:07.710
but we mustn't forget the
work that's been going on

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:10.260
to create the new NHS England.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:13.980
So we are on track to ensure
that by the 12th of December,

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:15.870
at least 95% of colleagues

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:18.960
know their future role in NHS England,

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:23.250
from January then to
March, continuing work

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:24.880
with any remaining colleagues

00:26:25.865 --> 00:26:27.180
to match them to available roles

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:30.210
and to complete the final
phase of the creation

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:31.950
of the new NHS England.

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:34.860
I think it's worth saying that
now the attention not only,

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:36.750
and Steve want to say more about this,

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:39.990
not only has gone on the process,

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.540
but really on the work
to build the culture

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:44.100
of the new organisation

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:48.750
and to create a sense of really seeing

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:53.700
how the value of bringing
HEE, NHS Digital, NHS England

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:57.000
as was TDA NHS improvement NHSX together

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.990
as one can really create
something new and better

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:01.710
in the way that you
were describing Richard.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:03.510
But it is hard work

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:06.330
and it's a tough time for a lot
of our colleagues right now.

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:08.637
So we will be introducing
a new orientation programme

00:27:08.637 --> 00:27:11.280
for all staff, which will help hopefully

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:13.020
to clarify our purpose, values

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:14.850
and ways of working as a new organisation.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:17.190
I think that will be
another important step

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:20.250
in creating that sense
of a new organisation

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:22.696
and my thanks to Steve and colleagues,

00:27:22.696 --> 00:27:25.590
but also to all of our
colleagues in NHS England

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:26.850
who have been going through this

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:30.060
for their continued
professionalism and engagement

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:32.310
and their continued focus
on doing what they can

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.440
to make sure that we're focusing

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:36.090
on our primary mission, which of course

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:39.180
is to support the NHS to
do their best for patients.

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:41.164
- Very good, Amanda, thank you.

00:27:41.164 --> 00:27:42.720
Now are there any comments

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.630
or reactions from the board
about anything that Amanda said

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.713
or anything that that I said,

00:27:47.713 --> 00:27:50.130
and we do want to pick up at
all, maybe on industrial action

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.150
or Ruth on industrial action

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:56.040
and reflections in terms
of preparation for it?

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.910
- Well I think Amanda covered
pretty much where our position

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:03.810
and how we're supporting
government with anything

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:07.680
that they need in their talks
and negotiations with the BMA.

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:11.550
I think Emily will cover
impact and preparation

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:12.663
- The preparation.

00:28:12.663 --> 00:28:14.490
- For the action.

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:17.700
- I would just like just
to reflect for a moment,

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:22.260
of course any additional
offer that will potentially go

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:26.610
to doctors could lead in
inevitably to a question

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:29.490
what that means for nurses and midwives.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:32.841
So that will be something no
doubt that will come through

00:28:32.841 --> 00:28:35.319
the Royal College of Nursing,
the Royal College of Midwives.

00:28:35.319 --> 00:28:36.980
- And we did see their reaction actually,

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:39.930
on the agreement offered
to the consultants.

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:41.612
- [Ruth] We did.

00:28:41.612 --> 00:28:44.400
- Sorry Andrew, yeah, please.

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:46.740
- Can I just pick up a point on costs,

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.060
what does it cost us so far

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:50.643
to cover the strikes this year?

00:28:52.698 --> 00:28:54.570
- To date the end of October,

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.580
our estimate is the direct financial

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:58.440
cost about a billion pounds.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:01.950
- And that's come out of resource.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:05.130
- Which has, well it has
come from a combination

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:08.541
of funding from the
government, the department,

00:29:08.541 --> 00:29:12.600
some redeployment of capital underspends

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:16.103
and some decisions again

00:29:16.103 --> 00:29:20.340
from the department to
rephase for example,

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:22.023
some of the technology programmes.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:24.960
- And there are indirect costs of course

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:27.240
not within the direct costs.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:30.420
- I mean the indirect costs
in terms of lost activity

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:33.237
in patient care of
roughly an equivalent size

00:29:33.237 --> 00:29:38.035
- We've rescheduled about a
million elective appointments.

00:29:38.035 --> 00:29:39.480
- I think we've rescheduled
probably a million appointments

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:41.482
and then there's a question of course

00:29:41.482 --> 00:29:43.559
of which appointments never
got booked in the first place.

00:29:43.559 --> 00:29:45.840
So there's a broader disruptive effect.

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.527
- Yeah, I get it, thank you.

00:29:47.527 --> 00:29:50.640
- If I could just, I mean this
is a hugely difficult area

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:54.030
and I know colleagues across
the board are working very hard

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:55.260
but to mitigate the impact.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:58.140
But is it worth, is there a way

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:00.360
of establishing from
this board perspective,

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:03.150
which is of course
looking at patient safety

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:06.450
and whether there's been an
impact, how that impact's been.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:07.920
Because as this goes on

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:10.678
and you know Ruth, if you're suggesting

00:30:10.678 --> 00:30:13.638
as they said, the nurses might continue,

00:30:13.638 --> 00:30:15.988
then what does this mean
for how we go forward,

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:20.680
- Amanda, Aidan, I mean
patient safety, do you want

00:30:21.729 --> 00:30:23.595
to try and pick up, I mean
how do we think about,

00:30:23.595 --> 00:30:24.897
clearly a very sensitive area

00:30:24.897 --> 00:30:28.650
but of a major disruption
to the NHS operations.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:30.480
So how do we think about that?

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:33.720
- So like others, I'm obviously concerned

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:36.840
about industrial action and
its impact on patient safety

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:39.450
and particularly at a
time where we would expect

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:42.118
to see significant winter pressures.

00:30:42.118 --> 00:30:45.000
I think there are several
things that are really,

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.903
really important to say.

00:30:46.903 --> 00:30:49.540
So firstly to recognise
the enormous amount of work

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:50.610
that colleagues do in stepping up

00:30:50.610 --> 00:30:53.970
and doing everything they
can to mitigate that risk,

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:56.063
they've worked incredibly hard.

00:30:56.063 --> 00:30:57.660
That particularly for the next round

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.714
of industrial action means

00:30:59.714 --> 00:31:01.512
our senior medical colleagues.

00:31:01.512 --> 00:31:05.160
The next point is that
we are clearly working

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.740
with the BMA and that
relationship is critical.

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:09.630
It's important that we maintain dialogue

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:12.570
about how we work together to minimise

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.280
the risk to our patients.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:17.100
Third important point
for patients is the need

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.230
to step forward when they are unwell

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:21.060
and need care regardless

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.082
of whether there's industrial action.

00:31:23.082 --> 00:31:24.240
We do not want people being put off.

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.850
We recognise that harm
is unequally experienced

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:28.860
by some of our communities

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:30.630
and there are particular communities

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:33.210
that we have feedback see this reported

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:34.510
and feel they have to care

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:36.443
for their own relatives
when actually they should

00:31:36.443 --> 00:31:37.276
be attending.

00:31:38.259 --> 00:31:39.773
- [Speaker] They should come forward.

00:31:39.773 --> 00:31:41.074
- So that's an important point.

00:31:41.074 --> 00:31:42.370
The other thing is that of
course this isn't the first

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:43.560
industrial action as
has already been said.

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:47.924
And so what we are doing is
looking at previous experience

00:31:47.924 --> 00:31:50.220
to see if there are any gaps

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:51.925
to see if there are any other
areas where we can do more

00:31:51.925 --> 00:31:54.390
to mitigate risk.

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:59.010
For example, making sure that
those particularly cancers

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:00.270
that are rapidly progressive

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:02.657
and require surgery are not delayed.

00:32:02.657 --> 00:32:04.560
In the world of transplant,

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:07.080
there's been a big focus
on solid organ transplant,

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:11.070
I think with less of a
focus on, for example,

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:12.510
corneal transplant.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:15.060
We've not been aware of
losing any tissues so far,

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:18.330
but it would be a tragedy
to do so because of this.

00:32:18.330 --> 00:32:20.280
So we're focusing on that.

00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:22.710
Ruth will probably want
to talk about maternity.

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:24.840
We need obviously to look at what goes on

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:27.480
in mental health and how we make sure

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.150
that patients don't miss
out on important changes

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:31.440
to their care because of it.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.370
And so there are a number of
areas that we can identify

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:38.400
where we will talk to the BMA

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:41.550
and others about how we support those

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:43.380
to make sure we're minimising the risk

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:45.900
based on previous experience.

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:48.030
I think the other thing,
and it's to this point

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:50.910
about people stepping forward
is we have from time to time

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:53.730
in industrial action
seen some unscheduled,

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:57.330
as an emergency care dropping
off, which is of concern

00:32:57.330 --> 00:33:01.290
for example in cardiac, if we
start to see that the levels

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:04.170
of attendance are dropping
off, that's an important point

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.360
for us to make sure our
messaging is very clear

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:07.860
that we are open for business.

00:33:08.816 --> 00:33:10.162
- Thank you.

00:33:10.162 --> 00:33:11.767
Do you want to comment
on maternity Ruth or?

00:33:11.767 --> 00:33:13.079
- I would thank you chair.

00:33:13.079 --> 00:33:16.290
So, and when you talk
about elective operations

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:17.340
could be cancelled

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:21.060
as a result of industrial
action, we'll need to make sure

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:24.200
that elective sections aren't cancelled.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:27.953
It's a tragedy for somebody
to wait an extra six weeks

00:33:27.953 --> 00:33:29.802
to have the hip replacement,

00:33:29.802 --> 00:33:33.180
but it'd be very different
having to wait an extra six days

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:34.530
for having an elective section.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:36.510
So these are really important.

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:38.970
We will work with a BMA on a daily basis,

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:42.420
hourly basis if we need to
ensure that doesn't happen.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:45.990
But this will need a lot of
effort from medical directors,

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:48.690
chief nurses, lead obstetricians,

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.420
lead midwives across England to ensure

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:53.550
that we keep women
babies safe during this.

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:56.822
But you know, medical directors,

00:33:56.822 --> 00:33:58.890
nurse directors are working
extremely hard through this.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:00.344
- And managers actually too.

00:34:00.344 --> 00:34:03.090
- They are really working very, very hard,

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:05.610
as well as all of our clerical teams

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.750
rescheduling and the like.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:09.930
So hats off to them 'cause
they work very hard.

00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:12.780
- Thank you, I think we should move on

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:16.890
into operational performance
update and more broadly

00:34:16.890 --> 00:34:20.070
and perhaps with also with
the focus of readiness

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:21.540
as far as we were able to be for winter

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:23.351
and what we're seeing.

00:34:23.351 --> 00:34:24.652
That would be really
good Emily, thank you.

00:34:24.652 --> 00:34:25.971
- Thank you.

00:34:25.971 --> 00:34:29.100
So the published paper
contains data for both October

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:33.330
and September, depending on
the area, given the time taken

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:35.871
to produce published
data in different areas.

00:34:35.871 --> 00:34:37.980
So I won't go through the whole paper,

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:40.650
which I'm sure people have looked at,

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.450
but just pick out a few of
the operational highlights

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:44.790
and then we will look ahead.

00:34:44.790 --> 00:34:46.470
So what we can see in the data

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:49.620
that October was indeed a
challenging month as Amanda

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:50.970
and Richard have both referenced.

00:34:50.970 --> 00:34:52.980
We had 1.4% more attendances

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:56.133
and 8.7% more emergency
admissions via A&amp;E.

00:34:57.717 --> 00:35:01.200
So we were proud that even given that,

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:04.484
the four hour ambulance
performance was at 70,

00:35:04.484 --> 00:35:05.850
A&amp;E performance was at 70.2%

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:08.040
and the category two
performance at 41 minutes

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:11.130
and 40 seconds, which is
nearly 20 minutes faster

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:14.160
than last year despite 7% more incidents.

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.170
So indicating as I think Amanda said

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:18.720
in her introductory remarks
that we are in a better position

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:21.974
going into this winter
than we were last winter.

00:35:21.974 --> 00:35:25.200
It's not just in A&amp;E performance,

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:26.941
emergency performance.

00:35:26.941 --> 00:35:29.334
So despite industrial
action in both September

00:35:29.334 --> 00:35:30.810
and October, the elective
programme was able

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:33.240
to make progress on some of our targets

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.121
while others sort of held steady.

00:35:35.121 --> 00:35:37.470
So 104 week waits

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:40.200
and 65 week waits basically held steady.

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:42.900
They decreased marginally
over that period,

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:47.520
but the 52 week waits
decreased by 5,521 waits

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:50.564
to 391,122.

00:35:50.564 --> 00:35:54.243
We do recognise however,
that over the same period,

00:35:55.162 --> 00:35:57.030
the 78 week weights have increased,

00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:59.010
in March, the end of March this year

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:00.660
we reduced that below 10,000,

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:04.280
which was regarded as
effectively being eliminated

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:07.260
and at the end of September
they were at 10,201.

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:09.540
So they'd gone up above 10,000 again.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:13.290
So I'll come back to the
planning work that the finance

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:15.325
and operational teams had been doing

00:36:15.325 --> 00:36:17.275
and talk about how we
propose to tackle that.

00:36:17.275 --> 00:36:21.180
On cancer, the urgent suspected
cancer referrals first seen

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:23.875
and the first treatments were 30%

00:36:23.875 --> 00:36:26.579
and 7% above pre pandemic
levels respectively.

00:36:26.579 --> 00:36:29.490
And the performance against
the foster diagnostics standard

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:32.820
was 69.7% in September.

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:36.090
That's fractionally below our
checkpoint, which was 70%,

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:37.920
but it's still within the modelled range.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.690
And we would continue,
depending on the impact

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:42.780
of I, of course to be confident in meeting

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:44.730
that goal at the end of the year.

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:47.400
And on diagnostics our
published stats show,

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.130
we are delivering record
diagnostic activity

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:51.600
above pre pandemic levels

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:54.303
and above the comparable
point in any previous year.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:58.500
So against that background
of performance improving

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:01.080
or at least holding
steady in critical areas,

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:02.880
particularly when we look at last year

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:04.410
on the 8th of November, we wrote

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.617
to systems clarifying the
areas of focus for the rest

00:37:07.617 --> 00:37:09.711
of the year that was
supported by the 800 million

00:37:09.711 --> 00:37:10.860
of additional funding that was provided

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:12.390
to partly respond to the financial

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:13.530
and operational challenges

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:16.560
that had already been
caused by IA this year.

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:18.060
Systems work within two weeks

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:20.040
to revise their plans and our finance

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.390
and operations teams have worked

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:23.820
to ensure essential goals
such as reducing the number

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:25.860
of those waiting 65 and 78 weeks

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:28.590
or more for treatment are
delivered wherever possible

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.390
while ensuring the absolute requirements

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.760
such as the maintenance
of UEC infrastructure

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:35.190
and plans have been protected.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:37.230
We've just finished, in fact
I think the team is doing

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:40.620
the last meeting now reviewing those plans

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:43.234
and agreeing delivery with systems.

00:37:43.234 --> 00:37:47.040
We are now in the first week of December

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:48.420
and we're already seeing,

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:52.104
seeing serious winter
pressures mounting, the data

00:37:52.104 --> 00:37:53.751
that's published this
morning shows the number

00:37:53.751 --> 00:37:54.584
of patients in hospital with flu

00:37:54.584 --> 00:37:56.250
doubled in the space of a week.

00:37:56.250 --> 00:37:58.380
Norovirus cases are continuing to rise

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:00.690
and they're up 15% on the week before

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:02.790
and RSV cases are also rising.

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:05.763
They're up 11% on last year and last week.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:09.630
I'm sure everybody knows somebody
who's unwell at the moment

00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:12.000
and it's likely we are going
to see pressure on the NHS

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:15.420
exacerbated as a result of
that in future weeks' data.

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:16.807
As we've all discussed,

00:38:16.807 --> 00:38:20.014
not just me, NHS staff have
been preparing for winter,

00:38:20.014 --> 00:38:23.003
frankly since the UEC plan
was published in January

00:38:23.003 --> 00:38:27.600
and we have a whole range of
infrastructure improvements

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:30.300
and investments in place
across every system.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.692
For example, we have more
call handlers in NHS 111

00:38:32.692 --> 00:38:35.970
and 999 services and we've
increased the number of beds.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:39.211
So every month through this
winter there'll be more beds

00:38:39.211 --> 00:38:41.372
available than there were
at the same time last year.

00:38:41.372 --> 00:38:45.060
So given this planning
and the replan that we,

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:47.255
as I said literally are
just completing right now,

00:38:47.255 --> 00:38:49.470
as everybody's already referenced,

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:52.620
it was incredibly
disappointing news to hear

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:55.426
that the junior doctors will
be on strike in the run up

00:38:55.426 --> 00:38:57.120
to Christmas and after Christmas.

00:38:57.120 --> 00:38:58.920
It's a major escalation

00:38:58.920 --> 00:39:01.500
and it is, as Richard said,
the worst possible time

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:03.288
of the year for this to happen

00:39:03.288 --> 00:39:05.700
and we are worried about
the impact on services

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:08.170
and patients, particularly
at this busy time.

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:11.280
We are already planning,
we've heard about some

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:12.750
of the clinical planning that's happening

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:15.660
to mitigate the risk to
patients as far as possible.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.850
And we know systems, we had calls

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:19.530
with the regions yesterday are already

00:39:19.530 --> 00:39:21.480
doing a lot of this planning.

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:26.480
But we will be writing out
so shortly, either today

00:39:26.768 --> 00:39:30.480
or tomorrow from our EPRR
team to lay out for systems,

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:32.490
the areas of focus, what the data

00:39:32.490 --> 00:39:35.190
that we need back from them
so that we can make sure

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:36.470
and ensure plans

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:39.086
so that they have all the available data

00:39:39.086 --> 00:39:39.990
and we have all the available data

00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:44.463
to know where risk is likely
to occur over this period.

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:47.610
What we are asking people to prioritise

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:50.160
will be the kind of maintenance activities

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:51.780
that always take place over Christmas

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.780
and think about each week
for the next five weeks

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:56.790
as having an obvious area of focus,

00:39:56.790 --> 00:39:59.220
particularly next week
for example, making sure

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:02.100
that any urgent appointments are scheduled

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:04.170
in the only week we've got now

00:40:04.170 --> 00:40:06.520
which doesn't have any
industrial action in it.

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:09.180
And equally the week between Christmas

00:40:09.180 --> 00:40:12.150
and New Year, which doesn't
have industrial action,

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:15.078
making sure that as far

00:40:15.078 --> 00:40:17.520
as possible services can
catch up in those weeks.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:19.710
The priority needs to be
urgent in emergency care,

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:21.930
including critical care, neonatal care

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:24.532
and maternity services,
as Ruth has mentioned.

00:40:24.532 --> 00:40:26.700
We know that strikes
means some appointments

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:29.160
and procedures are going
to need to be rescheduled

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:30.690
and we will only reschedule appointments

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:32.430
and procedures where necessary,

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:34.770
systems will try to rebook
immediately where possible.

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:36.970
But of course it may
not always be possible.

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:39.990
It's important to recognise the impacts

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.110
not just on routine care,
but it affects issues

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:44.580
like discharging patients,

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:47.280
which is critical to
keep the flow into A&amp;E

00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:49.200
and out of the hospital the other end.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:51.510
And there is a huge
admin burden which Julian

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.400
and others have referenced
in terms of setting up

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:54.510
and standing down work,

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:57.210
as well as the financial
pressure that that generates.

00:40:58.140 --> 00:41:00.690
So the major focus has to be
working with all our systems

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:03.660
to do everything we can
to protect patient safety.

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:06.187
There's no doubt it'll be very difficult.

00:41:06.187 --> 00:41:09.930
It'll put load on large numbers
of roles across the NHS.

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:12.090
We've already made a referenced lists

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:14.310
of those in this discussion.

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:16.710
I think importantly with all the systems

00:41:16.710 --> 00:41:19.800
is making the decisions in
line with clinical judgments

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:21.360
and making sure clinical
colleagues have the space

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:23.490
they need to make those decisions.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:25.100
Feel like we've probably, Aidan had some

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:26.700
of the conversation we
were going to have now.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:28.560
I dunno if you want to come in again.

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:29.760
- Yeah, I think we have covered that.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.070
Flow is of course critically important

00:41:32.070 --> 00:41:34.290
and where there is risk across the system,

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:36.090
we've got to make sure that's balanced

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:38.504
in the best possible way for patients.

00:41:38.504 --> 00:41:39.827
- [Speaker 2] Yeah.

00:41:39.827 --> 00:41:41.820
- So that will sometimes mean
perhaps earlier discharge

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:43.800
for patients in order to maintain

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:45.030
that flow at the front door

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:46.830
where we've got critically ill people

00:41:46.830 --> 00:41:48.420
and in order for them to be able to come

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:50.743
through into the system to receive care,

00:41:50.743 --> 00:41:53.250
I recognise those
conversations are difficult

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:55.873
and uncomfortable but they need to be had.

00:41:55.873 --> 00:41:56.873
- Thank you.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:01.770
- Thank you, any comments,
questions, reflections

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:04.353
for Emily on... Andrew.

00:42:05.318 --> 00:42:08.014
- So there's a hell of a lot going on.

00:42:08.014 --> 00:42:09.630
It's fantastic stuff

00:42:09.630 --> 00:42:12.090
and I pay tribute to the enormous effort

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:16.320
that's gone into getting
categories improvement.

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:20.070
Really fantastic, I'm
just thinking in terms

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:22.420
of the strike action going forward

00:42:23.281 --> 00:42:26.520
and whether or not we've
empowered our local systems

00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:30.480
to really push the comms in
promoting the alternatives

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:32.850
to A&amp;E because I think we've still got

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:36.090
a lot of patients pitching
up in an A&amp;E setting

00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:38.160
who don't need to be there.

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:40.860
They could be directed to alternatives

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:43.680
and I think given that it's
going to be a really difficult

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:46.380
kind of Christmas new year period,

00:42:46.380 --> 00:42:48.630
whether we could have a
concerted effort locally

00:42:48.630 --> 00:42:52.590
on local radio or the media
just to make that point.

00:42:52.590 --> 00:42:55.110
- Yeah, we've worked with
local systems on that

00:42:55.110 --> 00:42:57.870
and local systems are
usually very effective

00:42:57.870 --> 00:42:59.220
at particularly working with local radio,

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:00.693
as you say, Andrew,

00:43:01.543 --> 00:43:02.730
which is a really good way of
reaching local communities.

00:43:02.730 --> 00:43:06.720
So when a particular A&amp;E
is getting into difficulty,

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:08.340
they often will send a message out.

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:09.900
I think it's really
important those messages

00:43:09.900 --> 00:43:12.660
are in balance though
because we do have evidence

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:15.240
from previous strike in particular,

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:17.611
that people will just not go at all.

00:43:17.611 --> 00:43:19.770
So what we want is as Aidan

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.053
and others have said, if
you need to go to A&amp;E,

00:43:23.053 --> 00:43:25.620
but we need, as you say, to
make sure people are aware

00:43:25.620 --> 00:43:27.360
of given the need that they've got,

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:29.673
where should they go at the same time?

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:34.440
- Any other yes, sorry Mary.

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:35.540
- I think on this one,

00:43:36.504 --> 00:43:37.887
as it's kind of in the
lead up to Christmas,

00:43:37.887 --> 00:43:41.010
we know that for some people
mental health problems

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:44.298
are exacerbated, sometimes
associated with loneliness.

00:43:44.298 --> 00:43:49.298
So anything we can get ICBs to do

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:52.890
to encourage hubs as we did last winter

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:57.420
in things like church halls and other,

00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:00.510
and libraries actually I
think we could really alert

00:44:00.510 --> 00:44:01.710
how important it would be

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:05.910
to do it from the 20th
rather than the 23rd.

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:07.835
- Yeah, thank you, there's something

00:44:07.835 --> 00:44:09.090
we'll pick up with ICBs.

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:12.420
- Where do we think,
because I know that Emily,

00:44:12.420 --> 00:44:14.340
you and with Julian and colleagues,

00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:18.270
I mean you've met in
the last several weeks

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:22.290
with all of the 42 systems

00:44:22.290 --> 00:44:24.150
and have formed an assessment

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:27.650
clearly not just of just
the financial pressure

00:44:27.650 --> 00:44:29.292
and of actually the operational pressures

00:44:29.292 --> 00:44:33.540
and without sort of getting
into the details of which ones,

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:37.800
but to what extent do you
think we are able to help

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:41.400
those that are most challenged
and what are we doing?

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:43.334
And then there's a separate thought

00:44:43.334 --> 00:44:46.898
just on discharge into social care.

00:44:46.898 --> 00:44:48.825
I know that either Better Care Fund

00:44:48.825 --> 00:44:51.180
then with local authorities
I know with government

00:44:51.180 --> 00:44:53.970
has very helpfully given further
funding, I think 40 million

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:58.041
or so available to be tailored to those

00:44:58.041 --> 00:45:01.800
via the relevant authorities
into the systems which have

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:05.220
seen most challenging
in getting discharged.

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:08.490
And your comment on that would be helpful.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:13.490
- Thank you, so the additional funding

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:15.420
that's been provided over last winter

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:18.240
and into this winter for social care,

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:21.210
but in particular the more joint working

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:22.738
in every system between NHS staff

00:45:22.738 --> 00:45:27.738
and care staff has
resulted in better flow.

00:45:28.643 --> 00:45:32.880
It's very hard to quantify
exactly what impact it's had,

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:34.710
but we know from feedback on the ground

00:45:34.710 --> 00:45:36.570
from the discharge hubs for example

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:39.180
that I visited, that that's
an incredibly effective

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:40.470
way of doing things.

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:42.480
There is always more that needs to be done

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:45.540
and there is some places
where local authorities

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:47.130
are under financial pressure

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:48.840
where it's been harder to deliver that

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:50.795
over the last few months than it

00:45:50.795 --> 00:45:52.099
was last winter for example.

00:45:52.099 --> 00:45:53.430
But everybody locally is always

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:55.110
incredibly strongly committed

00:45:55.110 --> 00:45:56.755
to making that happen.

00:45:56.755 --> 00:46:00.270
In terms of your first question,

00:46:00.270 --> 00:46:03.600
my taking has perhaps
been helpful coming back,

00:46:03.600 --> 00:46:06.210
I've been back just six weeks now I think

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:09.224
and going straight into these
meetings with every system

00:46:09.224 --> 00:46:12.480
is that compared to where
everybody was last year,

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:15.568
every system is in a better
place than it was last year.

00:46:15.568 --> 00:46:19.440
And the tailored and
incredibly high energy

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:20.580
and focused support that's been

00:46:20.580 --> 00:46:23.183
provided to the systems
that were in tier one

00:46:23.183 --> 00:46:25.710
on the UEC programme has.

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:27.577
- Tier one being our most challenge.

00:46:27.577 --> 00:46:28.894
- Our most challenge systems

00:46:28.894 --> 00:46:30.766
has absolutely resulted
in major improvements

00:46:30.766 --> 00:46:32.310
in those systems,

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:35.730
but if we look at the spread
of performance last winter,

00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:38.760
if we move everybody up, that
still leaves some systems

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:40.860
who are definitely in a better position,

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:42.030
but it's still fragile.

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:45.870
So as we saw in London over last weekend

00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:48.690
where response times deteriorated

00:46:48.690 --> 00:46:52.770
due to a enormous increase
in volume of calls

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:56.400
and attendances, it did
result in performance slipping

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:59.276
in a way that I know the London team

00:46:59.276 --> 00:47:00.109
absolutely didn't want to happen.

00:47:00.109 --> 00:47:02.340
The system was able to
recover though more quickly

00:47:02.340 --> 00:47:04.263
than it would've done last winter.

00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:08.460
That in a system which is more
fragile to start off with,

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:09.960
both has a more negative impact

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:11.700
and is harder to recover from.

00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:13.980
So what we're focused on
is we know the systems

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:15.660
that are going to have the hardest time

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:18.300
and focusing on helping them
put in place the operation

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:19.620
improvements that we know work.

00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:21.660
So we've seen tremendous
improvements, for example,

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:25.187
of people adopting minimal
waiting times for ambulances

00:47:25.187 --> 00:47:28.590
and you know, Norfolk

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:31.336
and Norwich for example is
has in the last three weeks

00:47:31.336 --> 00:47:32.340
seen a dramatic improvement in performance

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:35.190
through putting in place
better flow procedures

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:37.770
through A&amp;E and into the hospital trust

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:39.420
adopting the north Bristol model

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:41.958
of moving people through
the hospital, et cetera.

00:47:41.958 --> 00:47:42.791
We know stuff works,

00:47:42.791 --> 00:47:46.530
but it's going to take
every day, every week

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:49.170
hand to hand-to-hand attention
to particular systems

00:47:49.170 --> 00:47:51.330
to keep everything working at this pace.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:55.101
And obviously the IA pressure
then just makes that closer

00:47:55.101 --> 00:47:56.490
to the edge and more challenging.

00:47:56.490 --> 00:47:59.490
- So that picture you just
painted of sort of London

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:01.830
and so it is hit by a surge.

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:03.450
- [Speaker 2] Yes.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:05.973
- It can't deal with the
surge and holter levels,

00:48:07.285 --> 00:48:08.630
but actually it recovered faster

00:48:08.630 --> 00:48:10.794
than we did last year, is that your point?

00:48:10.794 --> 00:48:12.520
- I think it's not fair
to say it didn't hold to,

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:14.045
it wasn't able to deliver the performance.

00:48:14.045 --> 00:48:14.880
We'd want to sub 30 minutes.

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:17.209
Yeah, but it, so it hit longer wait times,

00:48:17.209 --> 00:48:18.570
but it recovered within a 48 hour period

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:21.589
back to performance that
we would be expecting.

00:48:21.589 --> 00:48:22.899
- [Richard] Okay.

00:48:22.899 --> 00:48:25.230
- And we were able to spot

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:27.240
because everybody at the system level

00:48:27.240 --> 00:48:29.160
and at national level,
we have much better data

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:32.155
and much more immediate data
so that we can spot the problem

00:48:32.155 --> 00:48:34.290
and provide the support
that the systems need

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:36.540
or the system can address
its own challenges.

00:48:38.369 --> 00:48:39.202
- Okay, any other,

00:48:39.202 --> 00:48:42.900
I'm going to ask Julian
a very unfair question.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:46.830
It's sort of like a Christmas
cracker question quiz,

00:48:46.830 --> 00:48:48.540
slightly lifting the mood.

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:51.100
We refer to 12 gambling centres

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:56.280
and it's probably not one for
now, but who pays for those?

00:48:56.280 --> 00:48:59.820
I mean the gambling
industry makes an absolute,

00:48:59.820 --> 00:49:02.520
who pays for the gambling
centres that provides support

00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:03.910
to patients when they have

00:49:05.116 --> 00:49:07.080
real gambling problems?

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:08.040
Do you know that,

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:09.780
is that a fair question?

00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:11.850
- Than the answer.

00:49:11.850 --> 00:49:13.260
I think the answer is but
actually get it wrong.

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:15.990
I'll come back and I will confirm

00:49:15.990 --> 00:49:18.780
that the funding arrangements are in place

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:20.940
and the support we do
get, I think including

00:49:22.810 --> 00:49:24.155
from industry in support.

00:49:24.155 --> 00:49:24.988
- Do we, there is some
levy on the industry,

00:49:24.988 --> 00:49:28.246
but whether it's enough to
cover actually the level of care

00:49:28.246 --> 00:49:31.410
that we then have to provide
to their customer, anyway,

00:49:31.410 --> 00:49:34.110
I'm getting outside policy
aren't I, so we'll have to.

00:49:35.581 --> 00:49:36.930
- Richard if I may,

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:38.730
in these conversations we often have

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:41.830
a focus on acute care and primary care

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:45.060
and I do wonder at a future
meeting whether we might spend

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:48.660
a bit more time on mental
health made reference

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:51.390
to people who might be on sections

00:49:51.390 --> 00:49:54.060
who might not get the
attention they're require

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:56.283
during the junior doctor's action.

00:49:57.463 --> 00:49:59.820
And I do think it is worth
spending some time on that.

00:49:59.820 --> 00:50:03.780
I was also fascinated by the
increasing numbers of people

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:06.480
with autism in hospital

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:09.930
and I thought, I don't quite understand

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:11.700
what might be going on there

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:15.240
and I do understand what
we've been trying to do

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:17.580
in terms of caring for people

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:19.260
as often as possible in the community

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:23.760
as opposed to in long stay or in hospital.

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:27.153
But I just wonder whether
there might be some merit in.

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:30.907
- [Richard] So you're
going to be able deep dive.

00:50:30.907 --> 00:50:32.615
- More time on those kind of issues.

00:50:32.615 --> 00:50:34.800
- Simon, you presumably
absolutely agree with that.

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:35.633
- Yeah, I do.

00:50:36.679 --> 00:50:37.733
Yeah, yeah, we should.

00:50:39.447 --> 00:50:41.244
I mean we're overwhelmed
now with referrals

00:50:41.244 --> 00:50:42.544
for autism and ADHD assessments

00:50:42.544 --> 00:50:47.220
and the waiting list is
dramatically increasing almost

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:49.170
on a monthly basis for

00:50:49.170 --> 00:50:51.723
and we're not managing that at all.

00:50:52.819 --> 00:50:54.130
- I mean,

00:50:54.130 --> 00:50:55.170
I try and carry these stats in my head,

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:56.270
but you'll correct me.

00:50:57.200 --> 00:50:58.886
So we had a 39% increase in children

00:50:58.886 --> 00:50:59.719
and young people's presentations

00:50:59.719 --> 00:51:01.280
and mental health conditions in 22

00:51:02.297 --> 00:51:04.390
and I think it's 25% of 17

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:06.883
to 19 year olds we now believe have.

00:51:06.883 --> 00:51:08.259
- You're much more accurate than I an.

00:51:08.259 --> 00:51:09.632
- Well I suspect I'm not Simon.

00:51:09.632 --> 00:51:11.913
- Again another 50%,

00:51:11.913 --> 00:51:14.020
- But it was one in 10 17

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:16.050
to 19 year olds were
actually were presented

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:19.770
with some mental health condition in 2017.

00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:21.150
And it's now one in four.

00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:22.830
- There's two separate
things going on there.

00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:25.230
One is the actual prevalence of disorders,

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:27.791
which is gone up in children

00:51:27.791 --> 00:51:28.980
and young people, mainly young women,

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:32.100
16 to 24 gone up around six to 7%,

00:51:32.100 --> 00:51:34.650
which is actually very
big in population terms.

00:51:34.650 --> 00:51:36.780
The other is people coming forward,

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:38.220
which is dramatically increased

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:39.986
and they're very different things,

00:51:39.986 --> 00:51:44.280
including in adults as
well with neurodiversity

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:46.620
or ADHD when the
prevalence hasn't changed,

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:48.123
but the criteria have changed.

00:51:49.410 --> 00:51:51.750
- I think it's well
worth, as you say, David,

00:51:51.750 --> 00:51:53.010
a deep dive.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:54.170
So can I move us on

00:51:54.170 --> 00:51:56.370
to financial performance,
having given Julian

00:51:56.370 --> 00:51:59.301
an easy first question
on gambling centres,

00:51:59.301 --> 00:52:02.457
Julian, the financial
position for month seven.

00:52:02.457 --> 00:52:07.457
- So, just by way of context,

00:52:07.770 --> 00:52:10.420
let's remind that earlier,

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:16.403
well at the beginning of last month,

00:52:17.381 --> 00:52:19.855
about a month ago, as as we
described at the beginning,

00:52:19.855 --> 00:52:20.790
we have injected 800 million pounds

00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:23.271
of new funding into systems

00:52:23.271 --> 00:52:28.170
and have reduced the sort of
elective activity threshold

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:29.310
we expect people to do

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:33.480
before we will top them
up with extra income.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:36.302
Those two steps
effectively cover the cost,

00:52:36.302 --> 00:52:40.050
the impact or the financial
impact, not the activity impact,

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:42.038
but the financial impact of strikes

00:52:42.038 --> 00:52:45.963
up to the end of October.

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:52.380
As a result, when you
play through the impact

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.890
of that and the flexibility
that people are using,

00:52:55.890 --> 00:52:57.810
I mean to cover what I'll
come onto, which is some

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:59.593
of the kind of underlying
inflation pressures

00:52:59.593 --> 00:53:00.751
that also exist,

00:53:00.751 --> 00:53:04.408
the table in the paper
shows that the overspend

00:53:04.408 --> 00:53:09.408
against plan up to the end
of October is £400 million.

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:13.494
And people will note that as a lot less

00:53:13.494 --> 00:53:17.010
than it was in the report
we shared last month.

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:20.340
And that is because we have now
effectively covered the cost

00:53:20.340 --> 00:53:23.339
of industrial action that
still shows the degree

00:53:23.339 --> 00:53:25.230
to which people are behind plan.

00:53:25.230 --> 00:53:29.160
As we went through month seven,
that is roughly equivalent

00:53:29.160 --> 00:53:31.893
to probably where we were,
in fact probably last year

00:53:31.893 --> 00:53:32.726
we were a bit further off.

00:53:32.726 --> 00:53:35.620
However, one of the
reasons for that position

00:53:36.600 --> 00:53:41.600
is that we are still dealing
with much higher inflation.

00:53:43.500 --> 00:53:47.880
So the data published

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:51.453
by the OBR and the
government in the budget,

00:53:52.290 --> 00:53:54.720
or sorry not the budget
in the autumn statement,

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.770
showed that we outturned on
the GDP deflator measure,

00:53:58.770 --> 00:54:03.770
we outturned 22, 23 at 6.7% inflation.

00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:05.580
Now just remembering 12 months ago

00:54:05.580 --> 00:54:08.100
the forecast for the
end of 22, 23 was 4.4.

00:54:08.100 --> 00:54:10.050
So it's about one and a half

00:54:10.050 --> 00:54:12.188
to two percentage points higher.

00:54:12.188 --> 00:54:15.180
And currently the forecast for this year

00:54:15.180 --> 00:54:18.750
on the same measure is 6.1%.

00:54:18.750 --> 00:54:21.630
That is about four
percentage points higher

00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:22.980
than the forecast last autumn.

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:26.700
And the basis on which we were funded

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:29.861
and budgeted for the NHS

00:54:29.861 --> 00:54:33.970
and those two things combine
to create sort of a recurrent

00:54:35.308 --> 00:54:39.720
unfunded pressure of
about 1.7 billion pounds,

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:42.000
which we are dealing with.

00:54:42.000 --> 00:54:43.817
We will have to deal
with on a recurrent basis

00:54:43.817 --> 00:54:48.817
as that we then look forward to 24, 25.

00:54:49.358 --> 00:54:51.270
I mean that is a really significant cost.

00:54:51.270 --> 00:54:55.053
That is just the none pay inflation cost.

00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:59.280
And we see it as I've described
in previous board meetings,

00:54:59.280 --> 00:55:04.110
in medicines prices,
continuing healthcare costs,

00:55:04.110 --> 00:55:07.890
and just the kind of basic
contractual service costs

00:55:07.890 --> 00:55:10.965
that our organisations are facing.

00:55:10.965 --> 00:55:12.850
PFI costs energy bills now

00:55:12.850 --> 00:55:17.130
it's worth just putting it in context,

00:55:17.130 --> 00:55:18.810
when you look at those inflation figures

00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:21.720
and I think the IFS, the Kings Fund

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:24.117
and others have made these points,

00:55:24.117 --> 00:55:28.200
it means that the
average real terms growth

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:33.200
in NHS funding from 2019, 20 to 2024, 25

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:39.180
before you look at whatever the outcome is

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:41.842
of the consultant pay
deals and things like that,

00:55:41.842 --> 00:55:46.842
is for average real
terms funding will grow

00:55:47.940 --> 00:55:50.430
by just over 2% a year in real terms.

00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:52.380
So over that five year period,

00:55:52.380 --> 00:55:54.510
when we set off on the long-term plan,

00:55:54.510 --> 00:55:56.727
we thought the funding would be at three

00:55:56.727 --> 00:55:58.139
and a half percent real.

00:55:58.139 --> 00:56:01.110
So it's just over 2% real,
that's roughly the same

00:56:01.110 --> 00:56:06.110
as NHS funding growth the
years 2015, 16 to 2018, 19.

00:56:07.410 --> 00:56:09.240
And in the middle of that
we've clearly had to deal

00:56:09.240 --> 00:56:13.951
with the pandemic and
with now the recovery

00:56:13.951 --> 00:56:18.951
from the pandemic, which
I think just shows why

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:22.380
the performance pressures on the ground

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:24.960
when you look at the funding
and the operating conditions,

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:28.170
industrial action this year
are as hard as they are,

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:32.670
even with the improvements
that we have made.

00:56:32.670 --> 00:56:34.740
I mean that translates into, effectively,

00:56:34.740 --> 00:56:36.000
we absorbed a 4% real,

00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:40.350
I mean close on a 4%
real terms cut in 22, 23

00:56:40.350 --> 00:56:42.360
flat real funding this year.

00:56:42.360 --> 00:56:45.750
And it looks like it'll be slightly better

00:56:45.750 --> 00:56:49.920
than flat reel next if the
inflation forecast holds good.

00:56:49.920 --> 00:56:53.332
So just worth having that as context,

00:56:53.332 --> 00:56:55.766
clearly we are doing,

00:56:55.766 --> 00:56:59.910
we've asked every system now
to re-forecast its position.

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:02.250
We are pulling all of that
together that will still show

00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:05.310
that we have a pressure
that we have to deal with.

00:57:05.310 --> 00:57:08.430
It doesn't just immediately
turn around and reflects

00:57:08.430 --> 00:57:11.370
some of that underlying inflation pressure

00:57:11.370 --> 00:57:15.660
which we are taking steps to manage.

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:17.460
But those steps in local systems

00:57:17.460 --> 00:57:21.840
include money we had
intended for improvement

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:24.060
and transformation of certain services

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:26.250
is inevitably going just to cover

00:57:26.250 --> 00:57:29.703
some of that kind of basic cost pressure.

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:35.520
And December and January
strikes will clearly

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:38.460
put further financial pressure
as well as service pressure,

00:57:38.460 --> 00:57:40.960
which we will be in
discussion with the department

00:57:41.896 --> 00:57:42.729
and government as to how we deal

00:57:42.729 --> 00:57:46.410
with that over the next days and weeks.

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:48.029
But that's where we are,

00:57:48.029 --> 00:57:51.480
notwithstanding the impact
of the strikes to come,

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:52.440
we're still forecasting

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:57.363
therefore to live within the
funding we have been given,

00:58:00.240 --> 00:58:03.630
except for, as I said,
whatever is the final result

00:58:03.630 --> 00:58:06.660
of any further pay settlements.

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:09.180
For example, if the
consultants accept the deal

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:10.500
that has been put on offer.

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:12.543
So all of that being worked through.

00:58:14.640 --> 00:58:15.780
- Questions or comments.

00:58:15.780 --> 00:58:18.330
So Jeremy and then Wol.

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:20.580
- Yeah clearly a lot of pressure

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:22.640
and some very tight short term pressure.

00:58:22.640 --> 00:58:27.316
I just wonder over time
where you see the opportunity

00:58:27.316 --> 00:58:29.640
for productivity to play to
help relieve, maybe not now,

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:32.130
but in the future, whether there's a role

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:34.530
for technology in helping with that.

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:37.740
And if there is, how do
we create the capacity

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:39.661
for investing in technology

00:58:39.661 --> 00:58:41.123
when the current pressures are so tight?

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:46.410
- I mean we think technology
has a massive role to play,

00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:50.790
both in the sense of how
do you create better,

00:58:50.790 --> 00:58:53.790
more efficient standardised
processes through it?

00:58:53.790 --> 00:58:58.320
How can you create better
communication with patients?

00:58:58.320 --> 00:59:00.600
How can you put more of the
responsibility, you know,

00:59:00.600 --> 00:59:05.340
actually give patients the tools directly

00:59:05.340 --> 00:59:08.126
for care rather than always having

00:59:08.126 --> 00:59:10.143
to have a face-to-face interaction.

00:59:11.220 --> 00:59:14.283
The join up of data we talked about

00:59:14.283 --> 00:59:16.380
that commitment and investment

00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:19.653
we have just made for the NHS,

00:59:20.956 --> 00:59:24.180
I mean, I think the sort of generative IA

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:25.800
large language models have the ability

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:28.560
to transform, actually I would say

00:59:28.560 --> 00:59:32.160
how much time we've got
individuals spending on admin.

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:34.158
I suspect that will be actually one

00:59:34.158 --> 00:59:36.030
of the biggest transformative
things we can do.

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:37.950
Lot to learn how that gets applied

00:59:37.950 --> 00:59:40.323
and how we do it safely
and all the rest of it.

00:59:41.850 --> 00:59:45.450
And despite the pressure
we are keeping going

00:59:45.450 --> 00:59:48.379
with what we think are the
foundational investments

00:59:48.379 --> 00:59:51.518
to make that possible,
we are still rolling out

00:59:51.518 --> 00:59:56.430
sort of EPRs in the frontline
digitization programme.

00:59:56.430 --> 00:59:59.010
We will still progress
with the sort of investment

00:59:59.010 --> 01:00:00.810
in data and joining up the data

01:00:00.810 --> 01:00:03.690
and the applications
that make that useful.

01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:06.480
We are still investing in the NHS app.

01:00:06.480 --> 01:00:09.344
We will need to see the pace
at which we can keep going,

01:00:09.344 --> 01:00:13.953
It remains a priority for us.

01:00:16.818 --> 01:00:19.920
- Wol, thanks Richard.

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:23.130
So you're painting quite a bleak picture.

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:26.820
I mean one which has been,
as you say, a tough picture

01:00:26.820 --> 01:00:28.440
in terms of people on the ground

01:00:28.440 --> 01:00:30.999
finding it very, very, very difficult.

01:00:30.999 --> 01:00:34.890
And that's this year, I
mean in the private sector

01:00:34.890 --> 01:00:36.210
we've been experiencing this,

01:00:36.210 --> 01:00:38.756
we've been responding in much the same way

01:00:38.756 --> 01:00:40.890
as you have in the NHS,
but we look to next year

01:00:40.890 --> 01:00:44.463
and we sort of go,
gosh, that doesn't look,

01:00:45.840 --> 01:00:48.165
you'd be quite hard pressed

01:00:48.165 --> 01:00:50.286
to bet it's going to get
materially better economically

01:00:50.286 --> 01:00:53.220
in terms of inflation and
interest rates and what have you.

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:55.950
How are you thinking about
that and what sort of message

01:00:55.950 --> 01:00:59.909
can we give to the system
about, about next year,

01:00:59.909 --> 01:01:00.742
which is not that far away?

01:01:00.742 --> 01:01:03.540
- Well look, we are working on

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:07.020
how we finalise budget
allocations, you know,

01:01:07.020 --> 01:01:08.940
baseline budget allocations for systems

01:01:08.940 --> 01:01:10.560
and the kind of rules, the framework

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:12.933
within which we are
going to be asking them

01:01:12.933 --> 01:01:14.790
with the government's agreement to work.

01:01:14.790 --> 01:01:19.790
As we look at next year, those
conversations are ongoing.

01:01:19.800 --> 01:01:23.520
We have put investment in this year

01:01:23.520 --> 01:01:26.400
to increasing capacity and the baseline is

01:01:26.400 --> 01:01:29.490
let's maintain that
capacity into next year

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:36.191
as we continue our recovery from COVID.

01:01:36.191 --> 01:01:37.584
And I just think we all need to recognise

01:01:37.584 --> 01:01:39.420
you don't recover from the shock

01:01:39.420 --> 01:01:41.640
that we had overnight.

01:01:41.640 --> 01:01:43.380
That takes time, we
can see it's happening.

01:01:43.380 --> 01:01:45.655
I mean, not withstanding strikes,

01:01:45.655 --> 01:01:47.280
we're doing more work
last year than we did.

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:50.425
Sorry, more work this
year than we did last.

01:01:50.425 --> 01:01:55.425
We can see improvements
in productivity measures.

01:01:55.440 --> 01:01:57.270
The fact that sickness
absence is coming down,

01:01:57.270 --> 01:02:02.270
that has been a major hit
to what we can achieve.

01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:06.150
The fact that retention
has improved this year,

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:07.770
not as much as you know, you always want,

01:02:07.770 --> 01:02:10.470
but that is improving.

01:02:10.470 --> 01:02:14.160
So we just need to keep going
with the journey of recovery

01:02:14.160 --> 01:02:16.380
and with the resources
that the government agrees

01:02:16.380 --> 01:02:19.800
to give us, just do the
absolute best that we can

01:02:19.800 --> 01:02:21.846
for staff, patients and taxpayers.

01:02:21.846 --> 01:02:24.090
- Very good.

01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:26.374
- Any other comments?

01:02:26.374 --> 01:02:27.685
Sorry Mary.

01:02:27.685 --> 01:02:28.860
- Just one, I mean the last section

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:31.230
of your report is on
the capital expenditure,

01:02:31.230 --> 01:02:35.760
but listening to your eloquent description

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:40.680
of inflation, do you see
a problem there as things

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:43.020
that have been started or are due to start

01:02:43.020 --> 01:02:45.573
or cost more than originally anticipated?

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:47.880
- Yeah, we are definitely seeing,

01:02:47.880 --> 01:02:50.430
I mean in particular in
the construction industry,

01:02:50.430 --> 01:02:51.900
we've definitely seen the price

01:02:51.900 --> 01:02:53.550
of basic materials go up.

01:02:53.550 --> 01:02:55.260
That does mean that some of the programmes

01:02:55.260 --> 01:02:56.670
we have started are costing more.

01:02:56.670 --> 01:03:00.405
I mean we are seeing that in some

01:03:00.405 --> 01:03:01.470
of the kind of big hospital rebuilds

01:03:01.470 --> 01:03:03.341
that are already underway.

01:03:03.341 --> 01:03:04.770
In fact, even those

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:06.960
that sort of have come
to or close to completion

01:03:06.960 --> 01:03:09.000
and some of the other
transformation schemes

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:11.756
and we're having to deal
with that clearly again,

01:03:11.756 --> 01:03:13.075
for every pound you spend,

01:03:13.075 --> 01:03:14.880
it just means it goes less far.

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:18.490
So it does squeeze your our ability

01:03:19.338 --> 01:03:20.760
to do other things.

01:03:20.760 --> 01:03:22.623
It's a fact that we are dealing with.

01:03:24.455 --> 01:03:25.765
- Thank you

01:03:25.765 --> 01:03:28.740
- Julian, thank you very much.

01:03:28.740 --> 01:03:30.900
I mean it is a tough position.

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:32.910
We should expect to be a tough position.

01:03:32.910 --> 01:03:35.760
We'll see it in the private
sector's tough position

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:39.840
inflation, which is sort of laying quiet

01:03:39.840 --> 01:03:42.000
for so long is not quiet

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:45.129
and everywhere people are
having to deal with that

01:03:45.129 --> 01:03:48.300
and thinking how to
manage their businesses

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:50.790
or their operations in the teeth of it.

01:03:50.790 --> 01:03:52.768
I mean there is actually isn't there,

01:03:52.768 --> 01:03:54.090
we we are going to see
some small additional costs

01:03:54.090 --> 01:03:55.980
coming through from
actually what was very good

01:03:55.980 --> 01:03:58.737
which was actually the
agreement on BPAG was,

01:03:58.737 --> 01:04:00.069
and now BPAs I think.

01:04:00.069 --> 01:04:01.375
And that will have what
a couple hundred million

01:04:01.375 --> 01:04:03.515
knock on into this year or something.

01:04:03.515 --> 01:04:07.470
- Our forecast for 24 25 is
that creates a baseline pressure

01:04:07.470 --> 01:04:09.300
of about a quarter of a million pounds

01:04:09.300 --> 01:04:12.210
relative to the scheme
we have been operating,

01:04:12.210 --> 01:04:15.240
which we are operating up until
the end of December, 2023.

01:04:15.240 --> 01:04:16.440
- It was good to see that agreement

01:04:16.440 --> 01:04:17.670
between government and the industry.

01:04:17.670 --> 01:04:19.582
- It is really good to have agreement.

01:04:19.582 --> 01:04:22.260
It's really good to know how
we are going to work together

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:26.070
and to create the basis
for, I mean, continuing

01:04:26.070 --> 01:04:30.180
to work on improving the
approach to research.

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:33.510
Clinical trials as well as
for the ability for the NHS

01:04:33.510 --> 01:04:35.373
to access groundbreaking treatments.

01:04:36.750 --> 01:04:39.450
- Very good, let's move on.

01:04:39.450 --> 01:04:41.850
I think we're going to
item five on the agenda,

01:04:41.850 --> 01:04:45.630
which is Alex, I think
it's a primary care access

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:49.110
recovery plan and it's an
update really on delivery.

01:04:49.110 --> 01:04:51.000
So Alex, you have the microphone.

01:04:51.000 --> 01:04:52.100
- Thank you very much.

01:04:52.993 --> 01:04:54.304
Thank you chair, good afternoon everyone.

01:04:54.304 --> 01:04:57.780
So I'd just like to take
you through the paper

01:04:57.780 --> 01:05:00.540
that we've got presented at
the board today on the delivery

01:05:00.540 --> 01:05:02.490
plan for recovering
access to primary care.

01:05:02.490 --> 01:05:05.550
You will recall this publication in May

01:05:05.550 --> 01:05:07.193
and Amanda Doyle gave the update

01:05:07.193 --> 01:05:10.063
just a few days later to the May board.

01:05:10.063 --> 01:05:12.540
And just as a reminder,

01:05:12.540 --> 01:05:15.690
the plan at the time was well
received, had good support

01:05:15.690 --> 01:05:18.090
of publication and we are now six months

01:05:18.090 --> 01:05:19.833
into this two year programme.

01:05:19.833 --> 01:05:22.994
We are continuing to have
good support for the plan

01:05:22.994 --> 01:05:26.430
and a significant progress has been made

01:05:26.430 --> 01:05:29.763
in the first six months
of the programme of work.

01:05:30.750 --> 01:05:31.950
I would just highlight,

01:05:31.950 --> 01:05:35.342
although Richard has has
highlighted this as well,

01:05:35.342 --> 01:05:38.430
this it's a challenging backdrop.

01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:40.110
So with primary care, much like the rest

01:05:40.110 --> 01:05:43.620
of the NHS is under significant pressure,

01:05:43.620 --> 01:05:47.490
but I would just really
point to the capacity

01:05:47.490 --> 01:05:49.230
and the workload that is happening

01:05:49.230 --> 01:05:52.290
through primary care in
general practise at the moment.

01:05:52.290 --> 01:05:54.600
More than a million
appointments every day.

01:05:54.600 --> 01:05:57.980
As Richard highlighted
in general practise,

01:05:57.980 --> 01:06:01.320
the paper itself reminds
the board about the fit

01:06:01.320 --> 01:06:03.840
of the plan into the broader context.

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:06.750
It references the fullest stock take.

01:06:06.750 --> 01:06:09.540
But I would say just again, as a reminder,

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:13.770
this plan is unashamedly
about improving access

01:06:13.770 --> 01:06:15.300
to benefits patients and staff.

01:06:15.300 --> 01:06:18.450
It doesn't cover those
broader parts of primary care.

01:06:18.450 --> 01:06:23.450
It's overarching ambition
really precisely around access,

01:06:23.610 --> 01:06:26.280
tackling the eight o'clock
rush and making it easy

01:06:26.280 --> 01:06:30.120
and quicker for patients to
get the help that they need.

01:06:30.120 --> 01:06:31.462
We have four key areas in the plan itself.

01:06:31.462 --> 01:06:36.462
For each of these areas and
their associated commitments,

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:41.010
we've got a very clear
programme of work in place

01:06:41.010 --> 01:06:45.465
with governance and across
NHS England into ICBs

01:06:45.465 --> 01:06:49.650
and out to frontline services as well.

01:06:49.650 --> 01:06:51.690
And the board sets out the progress

01:06:51.690 --> 01:06:54.480
in each of those four different areas.

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:56.940
So if I may, I'll just pull out a few

01:06:56.940 --> 01:07:00.123
of the key points in each of those.

01:07:01.380 --> 01:07:05.490
So firstly, in relation to the first area,

01:07:05.490 --> 01:07:10.390
empowering patients,
just to I guess reiterate

01:07:12.450 --> 01:07:13.710
some of the earlier points,

01:07:13.710 --> 01:07:16.886
we've made some really good
progress with the NHS app.

01:07:16.886 --> 01:07:20.730
So we've already achieved
some of those ambitions

01:07:20.730 --> 01:07:23.880
and we're on track for 90% of practises

01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:27.633
to enable prospective records
accessed by March, 2024.

01:07:28.545 --> 01:07:31.230
This has a big impact both
in terms of the convenience

01:07:31.230 --> 01:07:34.350
for patients, but also in
terms of the efficiency

01:07:34.350 --> 01:07:37.330
and effectiveness for practises as well.

01:07:37.330 --> 01:07:38.626
- Can I just pause you just for a moment

01:07:38.626 --> 01:07:40.800
'cause I just want to make a point again

01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:44.040
because I think we
rather glibly talk about,

01:07:44.040 --> 01:07:46.800
people rather glibly
talk about the NHS app.

01:07:46.800 --> 01:07:49.484
It didn't exist six or seven years ago.

01:07:49.484 --> 01:07:50.785
- [Speaker 2] Yeah.

01:07:50.785 --> 01:07:51.618
- It is utterly remarkable

01:07:51.618 --> 01:07:54.180
and transformational for
the way patients are able

01:07:54.180 --> 01:07:56.335
to access their primary care services

01:07:56.335 --> 01:08:00.210
and the more we we invest
in its functionality

01:08:00.210 --> 01:08:01.560
and increase its functionality

01:08:01.560 --> 01:08:04.417
and improve its ability
to cross between primary

01:08:04.417 --> 01:08:07.500
and secondary, so much better
will be our ability to engage

01:08:07.500 --> 01:08:10.650
with patients and give them the service

01:08:10.650 --> 01:08:12.300
that they need.

01:08:12.300 --> 01:08:14.310
And I just think we sometimes,

01:08:14.310 --> 01:08:15.870
it's sort of in the background,

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:18.799
it's the NHS app, but actually
it's transformational.

01:08:18.799 --> 01:08:20.883
And it's six or seven years old.

01:08:22.110 --> 01:08:23.957
Anyway, sorry to interrupt.

01:08:23.957 --> 01:08:24.990
- No, no, no, absolutely.

01:08:24.990 --> 01:08:27.872
I imagine every one of
us around this table

01:08:27.872 --> 01:08:29.760
has got it on their phone right now.

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:34.760
So just to go back to the
empowering patients, secondary,

01:08:35.400 --> 01:08:38.970
and a good segue actually around NHS app

01:08:38.970 --> 01:08:43.590
is around the self-referral
part of the plan.

01:08:43.590 --> 01:08:47.730
So we committed to expand
self-referral services

01:08:47.730 --> 01:08:52.140
by September and by September
we had over 600 services

01:08:52.140 --> 01:08:53.730
offering those self-referral routes.

01:08:53.730 --> 01:08:55.923
That's continuing to progress.

01:08:56.940 --> 01:09:00.198
We have, I think really excitingly started

01:09:00.198 --> 01:09:02.790
to see a real shift in relation

01:09:02.790 --> 01:09:05.011
to expanding community pharmacy services,

01:09:05.011 --> 01:09:08.040
recognising the role as community pharmacy

01:09:08.040 --> 01:09:10.080
as a clinical service.

01:09:10.080 --> 01:09:12.900
So at the beginning of this
month we met our ambition

01:09:12.900 --> 01:09:15.570
to expand blood pressure monitoring

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:17.760
and also introduced direct prescribing

01:09:17.760 --> 01:09:22.200
of oral contraception,
expanding that service.

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:26.070
We are now focusing in the
community pharmacy world

01:09:26.070 --> 01:09:29.610
on launching the Community
Pharmacy First service

01:09:29.610 --> 01:09:32.850
at the end of January, which
will enable pharmacists

01:09:32.850 --> 01:09:34.740
to supply prescription only medicines

01:09:34.740 --> 01:09:36.960
for seven common conditions.

01:09:36.960 --> 01:09:39.570
Key focus for the team
at the moment is working

01:09:39.570 --> 01:09:41.220
particularly with digital suppliers,

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:44.940
recognising the digital
complexity of the programme

01:09:44.940 --> 01:09:47.793
and connecting community
pharmacy and general practise.

01:09:49.105 --> 01:09:52.500
And in pharmacy, we will
also be supporting that

01:09:52.500 --> 01:09:55.620
with a public campaign in
the spring to really try

01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:58.530
to emphasise and explain to the public

01:09:58.530 --> 01:10:00.780
the sort of clinical
services that they can expect

01:10:00.780 --> 01:10:02.523
to receive in community pharmacy.

01:10:04.110 --> 01:10:07.020
The second area of the plan related

01:10:07.020 --> 01:10:09.030
to implementing modern general practise,

01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:11.770
and again, we've talked a bit about this

01:10:11.770 --> 01:10:13.571
in the board already, around the role

01:10:13.571 --> 01:10:14.850
of digital technology,

01:10:14.850 --> 01:10:16.890
in implementing modern general practise,

01:10:16.890 --> 01:10:20.280
we're making real progress
on digital technology

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:22.710
but also on practise transformation,

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:24.825
both in terms of support practises

01:10:24.825 --> 01:10:28.350
and training for staff as well.

01:10:28.350 --> 01:10:30.750
For the digital world,

01:10:30.750 --> 01:10:33.600
our focus at the moment
is on digital telephony.

01:10:33.600 --> 01:10:37.671
So we have now around 83% of practises

01:10:37.671 --> 01:10:40.560
with digital telephony in use

01:10:40.560 --> 01:10:43.770
and we're working with ICBs to ensure

01:10:43.770 --> 01:10:45.383
we have that in place

01:10:45.383 --> 01:10:47.468
across all practises by March.

01:10:47.468 --> 01:10:48.480
- And that digital telephony

01:10:48.480 --> 01:10:50.070
essentially it's multiple calls,

01:10:50.070 --> 01:10:52.740
it allows callback functionality

01:10:52.740 --> 01:10:54.030
it queues appropriately.

01:10:54.030 --> 01:10:55.935
Is that what it does?

01:10:55.935 --> 01:10:57.245
- Precisely, precisely.

01:10:57.245 --> 01:10:58.590
And so, and it means

01:10:58.590 --> 01:11:00.858
that patients won't hear

01:11:00.858 --> 01:11:03.600
an engaged tone anymore,
which is a kind of key.

01:11:03.600 --> 01:11:06.833
So yeah, all of those points call back

01:11:06.833 --> 01:11:09.393
particularly helpful.

01:11:10.470 --> 01:11:11.303
Linked to that,

01:11:11.303 --> 01:11:13.503
there's also a digital
procurement framework,

01:11:14.503 --> 01:11:15.870
which is just going through at the moment.

01:11:15.870 --> 01:11:19.590
We aim to have that launched
at the end of January

01:11:19.590 --> 01:11:23.490
and that will enable
practises to draw down

01:11:23.490 --> 01:11:25.020
from the procurement framework

01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:27.690
for some really high
quality online consultation,

01:11:27.690 --> 01:11:31.950
messaging and booking as
well to really improve

01:11:31.950 --> 01:11:35.240
the experience of both patients and staff.

01:11:35.240 --> 01:11:39.300
We have had good uptake
of care navigation,

01:11:39.300 --> 01:11:43.290
a key part of the plan
to train staff to be able

01:11:43.290 --> 01:11:48.290
to help patients get to the
right part of general practise.

01:11:48.390 --> 01:11:50.070
So good up uptake from staff.

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:52.980
And similarly in relation
to practises themselves,

01:11:52.980 --> 01:11:54.630
we have over 1200 now going

01:11:54.630 --> 01:11:57.123
through the general practise
improvement programme.

01:11:58.440 --> 01:12:03.071
The two final areas of the
plan building capacity,

01:12:03.071 --> 01:12:06.000
there are two kind of
key elements to this.

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:08.340
So firstly around additional staff

01:12:08.340 --> 01:12:10.710
and we have met the ambition

01:12:10.710 --> 01:12:13.560
around additional direct
patient care roles

01:12:13.560 --> 01:12:16.920
with 31,000 additional roles in place.

01:12:16.920 --> 01:12:20.100
We've been running a public
campaign since October

01:12:20.100 --> 01:12:23.310
to explain the new roles in
general practise to the public.

01:12:23.310 --> 01:12:25.740
And the second part of building capacities

01:12:25.740 --> 01:12:27.660
have been around additional appointments

01:12:27.660 --> 01:12:29.400
and we're confident on delivery

01:12:29.400 --> 01:12:32.378
of the 50 million more
appointments by March.

01:12:32.378 --> 01:12:34.680
The final area I just wanted

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:38.110
to draw out is cutting bureaucracy.

01:12:38.110 --> 01:12:40.170
We've made good progress on that,

01:12:40.170 --> 01:12:44.030
but a really key focus to
draw the board's attention to

01:12:44.030 --> 01:12:46.950
is the primary secondary care interface.

01:12:46.950 --> 01:12:49.800
ICBs have been reporting on their progress

01:12:49.800 --> 01:12:53.100
to their boards over the autumn

01:12:53.100 --> 01:12:55.890
and we're just in the
process of reviewing that.

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:59.310
But the opportunity to
improve how general practise

01:12:59.310 --> 01:13:02.730
and primary care and how
secondary care work together

01:13:02.730 --> 01:13:04.740
improve the care pathways

01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:08.580
and become more effective
across the two areas

01:13:08.580 --> 01:13:10.680
is going to be really important.

01:13:10.680 --> 01:13:12.461
And Claire Fuller,

01:13:12.461 --> 01:13:14.310
who spearheaded the Fuller reports

01:13:14.310 --> 01:13:16.813
joined by Dr. Stella Vig,

01:13:16.813 --> 01:13:20.405
will be working across ICBs

01:13:20.405 --> 01:13:22.260
on behalf of NHS England on this.

01:13:22.260 --> 01:13:25.630
Finally, just today we've announced

01:13:26.562 --> 01:13:28.342
as well the commitment

01:13:28.342 --> 01:13:31.530
for online patient
registration has been met.

01:13:31.530 --> 01:13:35.340
So we now have over 2000
practises using the service,

01:13:35.340 --> 01:13:37.110
which means that people are able

01:13:37.110 --> 01:13:40.380
to more easily register with the practise.

01:13:40.380 --> 01:13:44.580
So I think in summary, you'll
see in the plan good progress

01:13:44.580 --> 01:13:48.900
to date, really pleased how
well supported the plan remains

01:13:48.900 --> 01:13:52.112
and our progress on the key milestones.

01:13:52.112 --> 01:13:54.240
We appreciate that the upcoming

01:13:54.240 --> 01:13:55.770
milestones aren't without risk.

01:13:55.770 --> 01:13:58.680
There'll be a lot of work for us to do,

01:13:58.680 --> 01:14:00.681
but we'll continue our focus on that.

01:14:00.681 --> 01:14:03.510
But I think really importantly,
we are really beginning

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:07.860
to hear now about how access
and experience is improving.

01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:10.800
So as we go forward we'll
really want to understand

01:14:10.800 --> 01:14:12.300
and measure some of the benefits

01:14:12.300 --> 01:14:15.570
and impact that the program's
having once we've got

01:14:15.570 --> 01:14:18.570
some of the infrastructure into place.

01:14:18.570 --> 01:14:21.870
And finally, I think it's
just probably worth us saying

01:14:21.870 --> 01:14:25.590
thank you to teams across
NHS England and the ICBs,

01:14:25.590 --> 01:14:28.380
and particularly to primary
care, general practise

01:14:28.380 --> 01:14:31.590
and community pharmacy who have
really pulled out all stops

01:14:31.590 --> 01:14:33.390
to get us as far as we've got so far.

01:14:33.390 --> 01:14:37.503
So six months in, we've
got a lot to be proud of.

01:14:38.520 --> 01:14:40.384
- So Alex, I'm going to open up

01:14:40.384 --> 01:14:43.583
for questions or comments in a moment.

01:14:43.583 --> 01:14:45.960
I mean, thank you for a
very clear presentation

01:14:45.960 --> 01:14:48.360
and a very good paper

01:14:48.360 --> 01:14:50.581
and you brought it to life very well.

01:14:50.581 --> 01:14:55.581
I think there is clear
benefit from the focus

01:14:57.468 --> 01:15:01.010
that has been on this work on access.

01:15:01.010 --> 01:15:05.370
It's very strong and real
work on access partnering,

01:15:05.370 --> 01:15:06.930
as you say with community pharmacy,

01:15:06.930 --> 01:15:09.660
but also actually with primary care.

01:15:09.660 --> 01:15:12.240
I sort of reiterate what I said

01:15:12.240 --> 01:15:15.420
at the start actually, that primary care

01:15:15.420 --> 01:15:20.420
is doing 17% is is my belief
more appointments than it did

01:15:20.610 --> 01:15:24.180
pre the pandemic, which
is a remarkable lift.

01:15:24.180 --> 01:15:28.183
and again, we often
decided just the huge value

01:15:28.183 --> 01:15:30.780
of the contribution, not just by our GPs,

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:33.000
but by practise nurses and
physios and prescribers

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:34.650
and social prescribers, et cetera,

01:15:34.650 --> 01:15:39.630
who function in primary care for us.

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:40.980
I did want to make a comment,

01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:44.074
which is going to sound
rather jarring not to us,

01:15:44.074 --> 01:15:47.460
but I was irritated to
read the representation,

01:15:47.460 --> 01:15:50.190
I call it the
misrepresentation of a study,

01:15:50.190 --> 01:15:51.964
which was published by Oxford,

01:15:51.964 --> 01:15:55.260
some elements within
actually my old university,

01:15:55.260 --> 01:15:59.782
Oxford University around
actually virtual appointments

01:15:59.782 --> 01:16:02.670
and the headlines and front pages.

01:16:02.670 --> 01:16:04.680
I don't mind it, it's rather jarring,

01:16:04.680 --> 01:16:08.910
but actually rather helpfully
a a very capable journalist

01:16:08.910 --> 01:16:12.000
who read the report in
detail to actually draw

01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:16.260
to draw out that actually
what the study said

01:16:16.260 --> 01:16:20.550
was an important overall
finding from this study

01:16:20.550 --> 01:16:24.090
is an examples of death
or serious harm associated

01:16:24.090 --> 01:16:29.010
with remote encounters in
primary care were extremely rare.

01:16:29.010 --> 01:16:31.590
And that's not actually how
it's portrayed in the media

01:16:31.590 --> 01:16:33.120
and I don't mind sounding rather jarring,

01:16:33.120 --> 01:16:34.990
but sometimes there is a portrayal

01:16:35.976 --> 01:16:37.650
of what primary care does
and the way it's provided

01:16:37.650 --> 01:16:40.470
to patients, which is just wrong.

01:16:40.470 --> 01:16:42.360
My own personal experience
when I'm calling

01:16:42.360 --> 01:16:44.430
my annual blood tests and then I'm offered

01:16:44.430 --> 01:16:48.360
a phone call by my practise
to go through the results,

01:16:48.360 --> 01:16:49.530
my blood tests.

01:16:49.530 --> 01:16:53.226
That's brilliant, I don't
need to go back in to sit

01:16:53.226 --> 01:16:54.210
in front of the person to actually go

01:16:54.210 --> 01:16:55.950
through the list of the outcomes.

01:16:55.950 --> 01:16:58.770
So I think actually I just wanted to make

01:16:58.770 --> 01:17:01.130
that jarring point, but
anyway, there we go.

01:17:01.130 --> 01:17:03.060
At which point, I'm going to open it up

01:17:03.060 --> 01:17:05.070
for comments or questions.

01:17:05.070 --> 01:17:06.842
Munir then Andrew, then Wol.

01:17:06.842 --> 01:17:10.650
- Good to see all the progress.

01:17:10.650 --> 01:17:13.080
So I've got two questions.

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:15.090
The first is about blood
pressure monitoring.

01:17:15.090 --> 01:17:17.730
It's great to see that
this is getting started.

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:21.273
I run a blood pressure
clinic in secondary care.

01:17:22.410 --> 01:17:25.770
So there are a lot of people
out there who are wandering

01:17:25.770 --> 01:17:27.780
around with high blood
pressure who don't know it.

01:17:27.780 --> 01:17:30.680
So it's important we identify
them and it's easy to treat.

01:17:31.853 --> 01:17:32.686
I'm just wondering what happens next

01:17:32.686 --> 01:17:34.380
when the blood pressure
is found to be high,

01:17:34.380 --> 01:17:36.347
it doesn't necessarily mean

01:17:36.347 --> 01:17:37.740
that they've got hypertension,
they presumably have

01:17:37.740 --> 01:17:41.070
to be referred back to the GP
who has to do an assessment.

01:17:41.070 --> 01:17:43.020
So can you just comment on the capacity

01:17:44.174 --> 01:17:45.007
and access in terms

01:17:45.007 --> 01:17:47.820
of the GP then being
able to look after that?

01:17:47.820 --> 01:17:50.130
And the second question
is on pharmacy first

01:17:50.130 --> 01:17:54.270
and treatment conditions
such as sinusitis, et cetera.

01:17:54.270 --> 01:17:56.670
How are we going to make sure
that there is not overuse

01:17:56.670 --> 01:17:58.997
of antibiotics given the dangers

01:17:58.997 --> 01:17:59.970
of antimicrobial resistance?

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:02.262
- So can I, Alex, we're going to do,

01:18:02.262 --> 01:18:03.604
we're going to just take a couple more

01:18:03.604 --> 01:18:06.759
if you can sort of scribble
down what they are.

01:18:06.759 --> 01:18:07.592
And so Andrew.

01:18:07.592 --> 01:18:10.170
- So my point is in relation to capacity,

01:18:10.170 --> 01:18:12.960
so we've got another 31,000 people

01:18:12.960 --> 01:18:15.930
working in general practise.

01:18:15.930 --> 01:18:18.245
What was the criteria for allocating

01:18:18.245 --> 01:18:22.110
those individuals in the sense of,

01:18:22.110 --> 01:18:25.350
was it focused on poor performing

01:18:25.350 --> 01:18:28.020
general practise surgeries

01:18:28.020 --> 01:18:31.680
or was it deprived areas or was it just

01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:35.195
an even kind of spread of increase?

01:18:35.195 --> 01:18:38.280
- If we can take one more

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:41.310
before you deal with those Alex.

01:18:41.310 --> 01:18:43.560
- Very quickly, one question was on,

01:18:43.560 --> 01:18:46.950
you'd mentioned in implementing
modern general practise

01:18:46.950 --> 01:18:49.350
access lot about technology et cetera

01:18:49.350 --> 01:18:51.990
and interface with secondary care,

01:18:51.990 --> 01:18:54.360
but you didn't talk much about social care

01:18:54.360 --> 01:18:56.490
because that's the
other place where easing

01:18:56.490 --> 01:18:59.410
and the GPS access and
understanding what's going on

01:19:00.405 --> 01:19:02.984
and there's a lot of people
providing digital care records

01:19:02.984 --> 01:19:04.293
now for that whole area.

01:19:05.130 --> 01:19:06.450
And the second part

01:19:06.450 --> 01:19:08.883
of my question was on cutting bureaucracy,

01:19:08.883 --> 01:19:10.549
this is slightly unfair because you seem

01:19:10.549 --> 01:19:13.218
to have done quite a good
job already, the progress

01:19:13.218 --> 01:19:18.218
and what next because
there's always more to do

01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:20.970
and just because you solved this bit,

01:19:20.970 --> 01:19:22.890
actually you need to look,

01:19:22.890 --> 01:19:25.427
look at the next next wave of
things that need to get going.

01:19:25.427 --> 01:19:26.732
So if you could just give a flavour

01:19:26.732 --> 01:19:28.041
for that as well, thank you.

01:19:28.041 --> 01:19:29.338
- Do you want to have a go at those?

01:19:29.338 --> 01:19:30.171
- Yes, thank you.

01:19:30.171 --> 01:19:32.257
I might go in reverse
order on some of them.

01:19:32.257 --> 01:19:35.130
So cutting bureaucracy,

01:19:35.130 --> 01:19:37.680
that's a really important
point about what next.

01:19:37.680 --> 01:19:39.579
I think it's fair to say,

01:19:39.579 --> 01:19:41.010
and I'm sure the clinicians
in the room would agree

01:19:41.010 --> 01:19:42.540
that there is a huge amount more

01:19:42.540 --> 01:19:45.570
to do on primary secondary care interface.

01:19:45.570 --> 01:19:47.790
I think we're starting
to scratch the surface,

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:51.420
but I think that is an area
where we will really need

01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:53.520
to have a concerted effort.

01:19:53.520 --> 01:19:56.040
So I don't think that
will be solved immediately

01:19:56.040 --> 01:19:57.870
and it will be really interesting to see

01:19:57.870 --> 01:20:00.616
what the ICB plans say in
terms of progress so far,

01:20:00.616 --> 01:20:05.616
in relation to social care,
a really helpful point.

01:20:06.600 --> 01:20:10.920
Clearly as part of the teams
that work in practises,

01:20:10.920 --> 01:20:12.810
we've got more social prescribing

01:20:12.810 --> 01:20:15.084
and some of this I think links

01:20:15.084 --> 01:20:16.970
to Claire Fuller's work earlier as well

01:20:16.970 --> 01:20:18.030
around what the integrated
neighbourhood teams look like

01:20:18.030 --> 01:20:20.790
and how you have a community around people

01:20:20.790 --> 01:20:24.390
who are serving them that
are made up of the totality.

01:20:24.390 --> 01:20:26.370
So again, I think,

01:20:26.370 --> 01:20:30.210
for the access recovery plan,
it is focused on access,

01:20:30.210 --> 01:20:31.920
but there are those opportunities

01:20:31.920 --> 01:20:33.510
and we should make the most particularly

01:20:33.510 --> 01:20:36.690
of some of the roles in general practise.

01:20:36.690 --> 01:20:39.410
The social prescribing link
workers would be a good one.

01:20:39.410 --> 01:20:43.710
In relation to the additional roles.

01:20:43.710 --> 01:20:47.580
So we've had an additional
role reimbursement scheme

01:20:47.580 --> 01:20:49.860
that's been running now
for a number of years

01:20:49.860 --> 01:20:51.720
through the GP contract.

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:55.880
So practises have been
able to draw down from,

01:20:55.880 --> 01:20:58.770
as part of the contract for

01:20:58.770 --> 01:21:03.000
and appointing people,
firstly in accordance

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:06.660
to a list of professionals
that have been available.

01:21:06.660 --> 01:21:09.300
And I will check out precisely

01:21:09.300 --> 01:21:11.670
how they were identified
in the first place.

01:21:11.670 --> 01:21:14.680
And particularly your point
about health inequalities

01:21:16.230 --> 01:21:20.100
in relation to Pharmacy First, that point

01:21:20.100 --> 01:21:24.630
about anti-microbial
resistance has been a really,

01:21:24.630 --> 01:21:28.140
really important part of
the programme of work.

01:21:28.140 --> 01:21:30.900
I know Steve and particularly
being very involved

01:21:30.900 --> 01:21:35.010
with it over the last three, four months,

01:21:35.010 --> 01:21:37.500
along with Chris Witty

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:40.380
to make sure that we
are in a good position

01:21:40.380 --> 01:21:42.691
and part of the things that
we've really needed to look at

01:21:42.691 --> 01:21:47.280
is to really take into
account how do we make sure

01:21:47.280 --> 01:21:50.190
that we monitor well the prescribing rates

01:21:50.190 --> 01:21:52.752
and that they are within tolerances.

01:21:52.752 --> 01:21:56.250
Which then I think finally
relates back to the blood

01:21:56.250 --> 01:22:00.840
pressure monitoring for the
blood pressure monitoring area

01:22:00.840 --> 01:22:04.260
and indeed all of the
community pharmacy areas,

01:22:04.260 --> 01:22:06.240
it's been important to
be able to make sure

01:22:06.240 --> 01:22:10.740
that we've got a good technology
linking up primary care,

01:22:10.740 --> 01:22:13.320
general practise and primary
care community pharmacy

01:22:13.320 --> 01:22:17.684
to make sure that where action
needs to be taken, it is,

01:22:17.684 --> 01:22:21.390
and that it can be done
in a non bureaucratic way

01:22:21.390 --> 01:22:24.360
as well to make sure that we are able

01:22:24.360 --> 01:22:26.463
to treat patients when they need it.

01:22:27.900 --> 01:22:29.908
- So can I just ask,

01:22:29.908 --> 01:22:33.540
it's sort of the mind your eye comment

01:22:33.540 --> 01:22:36.840
in paragraph seven about pharmacy first.

01:22:36.840 --> 01:22:39.070
So family first is a brilliant idea

01:22:41.130 --> 01:22:42.921
and a really good engagement,

01:22:42.921 --> 01:22:45.720
but it's that last sort
of couple of sentences,

01:22:45.720 --> 01:22:47.290
delivery remains challenging

01:22:48.142 --> 01:22:50.070
due to the changes needed
in digital infrastructure

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:52.110
and we're working with suppliers,

01:22:52.110 --> 01:22:54.060
support journeys implementation,

01:22:54.060 --> 01:22:56.962
confidence that we're getting that

01:22:56.962 --> 01:22:59.340
sorted out or is it likely to be delayed?

01:22:59.340 --> 01:23:01.920
I mean, what's the essence of the problem?

01:23:01.920 --> 01:23:02.880
- [Speaker 2] So the essence

01:23:02.880 --> 01:23:04.650
of the problem is the complexity

01:23:04.650 --> 01:23:08.490
of connecting digital
suppliers in general practise

01:23:08.490 --> 01:23:11.010
and digital suppliers
in community pharmacy

01:23:11.010 --> 01:23:13.770
because we've never done that before.

01:23:13.770 --> 01:23:16.007
John may want to comment as well,

01:23:16.007 --> 01:23:19.290
but the team are tracking
this on a day-to-day basis

01:23:19.290 --> 01:23:23.100
with delivery confidence being looked at

01:23:23.100 --> 01:23:25.380
on a day-to-day basis as well.

01:23:25.380 --> 01:23:28.474
At the moment their focus is on testing,

01:23:28.474 --> 01:23:33.150
testing on first of type
and some of the consultation

01:23:33.150 --> 01:23:35.610
readout areas particularly.

01:23:35.610 --> 01:23:38.880
So I think on this one we will
need to continue to monitor

01:23:38.880 --> 01:23:43.380
and be clear about the
impact of the progress

01:23:43.380 --> 01:23:46.380
that they're making and
the position at the end of.

01:23:46.380 --> 01:23:48.571
- Is it something you can comment on or?

01:23:48.571 --> 01:23:50.443
- I mean, just to underline the point,

01:23:50.443 --> 01:23:52.552
it's a complex environment,
multiple suppliers

01:23:52.552 --> 01:23:55.260
and a fixed time scale.

01:23:55.260 --> 01:23:58.060
So I think it's right to be
cautious about the delivery.

01:23:59.250 --> 01:24:02.400
Everyone's doing everything
they can with daily calls

01:24:02.400 --> 01:24:04.230
and holding supplies to account

01:24:04.230 --> 01:24:06.900
and so we're monitoring it really closely.

01:24:06.900 --> 01:24:09.780
- Okay, thank you, are
there any other comments

01:24:09.780 --> 01:24:11.793
or reflections for Alex?

01:24:13.560 --> 01:24:14.880
Really good, thank you very much Alex.

01:24:14.880 --> 01:24:15.930
Excellent, thank you.

01:24:17.400 --> 01:24:19.710
So let's move on, I think it's item six.

01:24:19.710 --> 01:24:24.261
It's Navina, long-term
workforce plan update

01:24:24.261 --> 01:24:26.250
and this is a really, really important

01:24:26.250 --> 01:24:28.170
step forward for the NHS, so.

01:24:28.170 --> 01:24:29.260
- Thank you Richard.

01:24:30.140 --> 01:24:33.030
So it's five months since
the publication of the plan.

01:24:33.030 --> 01:24:37.560
So I've brought this
update to give the board

01:24:37.560 --> 01:24:40.950
a sense of the work that's been going on.

01:24:40.950 --> 01:24:45.240
We've picked some of
the key pieces of work,

01:24:45.240 --> 01:24:46.830
there's actually a lot going on,

01:24:46.830 --> 01:24:48.879
so this would've been a very long report.

01:24:48.879 --> 01:24:52.410
But we've kept it to what we feel

01:24:52.410 --> 01:24:54.480
is really important for the board

01:24:54.480 --> 01:24:56.425
to recognise at this stage.

01:24:56.425 --> 01:25:00.120
So I'm going to just pick on a few areas

01:25:00.120 --> 01:25:04.650
that I want to describe
in by way of introduction.

01:25:04.650 --> 01:25:08.550
First of all, in November we
hosted the first long-term

01:25:08.550 --> 01:25:10.407
workforce plan delivery summit

01:25:10.407 --> 01:25:15.407
where we brought together many
stakeholders to work together

01:25:15.810 --> 01:25:18.540
to implement the plan to
consider how we would do that,

01:25:18.540 --> 01:25:20.730
what are the challenges, how
are we going to work together

01:25:20.730 --> 01:25:22.620
to overcome them.

01:25:22.620 --> 01:25:26.436
We had representation from
around 100 organisations

01:25:26.436 --> 01:25:28.470
and it was a really good day.

01:25:28.470 --> 01:25:29.490
People stayed right to the end,

01:25:29.490 --> 01:25:31.440
which I think is always a good sign.

01:25:31.440 --> 01:25:34.530
And it was sharing what
were the thorny issues,

01:25:34.530 --> 01:25:38.310
what was already working, where
did we need to work together

01:25:38.310 --> 01:25:41.760
to deliver as a coalition
in the main areas

01:25:41.760 --> 01:25:44.220
of train retain and reform?

01:25:44.220 --> 01:25:46.110
And we were able to think about now

01:25:46.110 --> 01:25:49.710
and also hold onto what we
needed to do for the future.

01:25:49.710 --> 01:25:53.430
One of the overarching themes
we heard on the day repeatedly

01:25:53.430 --> 01:25:56.220
was how this plan gives people hope.

01:25:56.220 --> 01:25:57.840
And that was a really important,

01:25:57.840 --> 01:26:00.630
a precious thing for us to hold onto.

01:26:00.630 --> 01:26:03.960
And it was really an opportunity to think

01:26:03.960 --> 01:26:06.480
how collectively we could work

01:26:06.480 --> 01:26:08.610
to deliver a sustainable workforce

01:26:08.610 --> 01:26:10.650
and really hold onto the concept

01:26:10.650 --> 01:26:15.273
of more and different, which
is central to the plan.

01:26:16.110 --> 01:26:20.520
So we started to work on,
as we move into early stages

01:26:20.520 --> 01:26:23.700
of del delivery, how we
galvanise the service,

01:26:23.700 --> 01:26:27.900
how we galvanise all of our
partners across government

01:26:27.900 --> 01:26:30.570
in education, in our professional bodies

01:26:30.570 --> 01:26:31.680
with our regulators.

01:26:31.680 --> 01:26:35.460
There's a very long list to
actually do this work together,

01:26:35.460 --> 01:26:38.550
but each part can't do it on its own.

01:26:38.550 --> 01:26:42.270
And I think that's a
very important message.

01:26:42.270 --> 01:26:45.150
So there will be further
events in the coming months.

01:26:45.150 --> 01:26:47.130
We are going to have three planned

01:26:47.130 --> 01:26:50.040
where we are breaking down
the work in those areas.

01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:52.620
And so I'll be able to report back

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:54.540
to the board on our progress.

01:26:54.540 --> 01:26:56.910
In the meantime progress what is reported

01:26:56.910 --> 01:26:58.764
to the workforce training

01:26:58.764 --> 01:26:59.880
and education committee of the board.

01:26:59.880 --> 01:27:01.920
So I just want to pick
on a couple of areas,

01:27:01.920 --> 01:27:04.380
if I may, in train,

01:27:04.380 --> 01:27:06.982
we actually brought forward delivery

01:27:06.982 --> 01:27:08.640
of 205 additional medical
school places this year.

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:12.270
That was not part of the
original plan, but we did that

01:27:12.270 --> 01:27:15.600
because we thought we could and
government wanted us to try.

01:27:15.600 --> 01:27:20.600
So those places are to be shared
across five medical schools

01:27:20.610 --> 01:27:22.953
and we made sure they
were in the areas where,

01:27:23.798 --> 01:27:25.445
or areas of need in accordance

01:27:25.445 --> 01:27:28.050
with how the spirit of the plan.

01:27:28.050 --> 01:27:31.800
So that adds 205 places, addition

01:27:31.800 --> 01:27:34.260
to the existing 7,500.

01:27:34.260 --> 01:27:39.120
Another point to make is
1,106 students were recruited

01:27:39.120 --> 01:27:42.720
to our blended learning
pilots in nursing to date.

01:27:42.720 --> 01:27:45.060
And those blended learning
pilots are really important

01:27:45.060 --> 01:27:49.530
to help us inform and plan for
how we learn in the future.

01:27:49.530 --> 01:27:51.600
And we've already mentioned retention

01:27:51.600 --> 01:27:55.530
and we are very cautiously
proud of the work

01:27:55.530 --> 01:27:58.320
that is being done out
there in the service.

01:27:58.320 --> 01:28:00.799
Not here in NHS England,
but out there by our leaders

01:28:00.799 --> 01:28:04.480
and our staff in the service
to really tackle retention

01:28:04.480 --> 01:28:07.920
and not have our new staff

01:28:07.920 --> 01:28:09.960
just replacing people who are leaving.

01:28:09.960 --> 01:28:11.280
So that's a really important point.

01:28:11.280 --> 01:28:13.590
So the staff leaver rate is down

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:17.280
for the 12th consecutive
month this last in August.

01:28:17.280 --> 01:28:19.470
This means that there
are about 14,000 people

01:28:19.470 --> 01:28:22.276
who have stayed in their roles in the NHS,

01:28:22.276 --> 01:28:25.350
the People Promise exemplar programme.

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:28.560
We've seen really great improvement

01:28:28.560 --> 01:28:31.500
where we've tackled the leaver rate.

01:28:31.500 --> 01:28:33.700
And so now we are rolling out

01:28:34.749 --> 01:28:37.500
that programme further,
we've had more trusts

01:28:37.500 --> 01:28:39.810
wanting to be part of that programme.

01:28:39.810 --> 01:28:43.680
We've already mentioned
the 50K nursing target

01:28:43.680 --> 01:28:45.690
that was achieved, which
is a manifesto commitment

01:28:45.690 --> 01:28:50.400
through training through
recruitment and through retention.

01:28:50.400 --> 01:28:52.800
So all parts working really well together.

01:28:52.800 --> 01:28:57.330
In fact, we exceeded
the 50,000. In reform,

01:28:57.330 --> 01:29:00.342
I just want to mention two
areas, enhancing generalism,

01:29:00.342 --> 01:29:04.170
which is something that
is really important

01:29:04.170 --> 01:29:05.580
that we think through very carefully.

01:29:05.580 --> 01:29:09.090
So we've got seven regional trailblazers

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:10.890
piloting the new model

01:29:10.890 --> 01:29:13.456
for generalist skills training

01:29:13.456 --> 01:29:17.790
for postgraduate foundation
and core psychiatry.

01:29:17.790 --> 01:29:19.320
And that's what we're
targeting at the moment.

01:29:19.320 --> 01:29:22.230
So we'll begin to see what the
results of that looks like,

01:29:22.230 --> 01:29:23.580
what we're learning for it from it

01:29:23.580 --> 01:29:25.170
and how we want to spread

01:29:25.170 --> 01:29:27.540
generalism training and understanding.

01:29:27.540 --> 01:29:31.530
So can I just also go into one area

01:29:31.530 --> 01:29:33.150
which is hot off the press,

01:29:33.150 --> 01:29:36.120
which is the rise in
acceptance on medicine,

01:29:36.120 --> 01:29:39.060
dentistry and midwifery courses

01:29:39.060 --> 01:29:42.068
because we had some information from UCAS.

01:29:42.068 --> 01:29:46.380
The data published
today, in fact from UCAS

01:29:46.380 --> 01:29:47.760
shows that the number of students

01:29:47.760 --> 01:29:49.860
accepting places on medicine,

01:29:49.860 --> 01:29:52.772
dental and midwifery courses
has risen over the last year.

01:29:52.772 --> 01:29:56.130
But we actually want to
get more applications in

01:29:56.130 --> 01:29:57.840
and then we want to convert

01:29:57.840 --> 01:30:00.060
those applications into acceptance.

01:30:00.060 --> 01:30:02.490
And so we're doing some very detailed work

01:30:02.490 --> 01:30:05.880
with the university and education
higher education sector.

01:30:05.880 --> 01:30:08.340
'cause we've got another
opportunity in January

01:30:08.340 --> 01:30:10.740
to have a look at how we're doing

01:30:10.740 --> 01:30:13.710
and then do further
recruitment drives for students

01:30:13.710 --> 01:30:16.156
and they have to be really done locally

01:30:16.156 --> 01:30:19.350
as well as with our national campaigns.

01:30:19.350 --> 01:30:23.733
So that is another area of
target for us going forward.

01:30:24.870 --> 01:30:26.713
I think I'll stop there.

01:30:26.713 --> 01:30:28.230
I could go on.

01:30:28.230 --> 01:30:30.660
- Very good and thank
you for your leadership

01:30:30.660 --> 01:30:31.770
in this space actually.

01:30:31.770 --> 01:30:35.174
It's really important
and as I say thank you

01:30:35.174 --> 01:30:36.690
for your leadership and for the progress

01:30:36.690 --> 01:30:37.890
that's clearly being made.

01:30:37.890 --> 01:30:39.300
I might get David to comment in a moment

01:30:39.300 --> 01:30:42.270
because David and I went up to Lancaster

01:30:42.270 --> 01:30:43.720
and South Cumbria actually

01:30:44.864 --> 01:30:47.100
three weeks or so ago on a visit.

01:30:47.100 --> 01:30:50.367
And actually we had very good
interaction conversations

01:30:50.367 --> 01:30:52.110
about actually how do
you scale up training

01:30:52.110 --> 01:30:54.840
and particularly it's less,

01:30:54.840 --> 01:30:57.600
this is my lay person's view,
so it's going to be wrong.

01:30:57.600 --> 01:30:59.670
It's not so much the
opening of the places,

01:30:59.670 --> 01:31:02.490
it's actually then giving
a practical experience

01:31:02.490 --> 01:31:04.800
and training placed in the system.

01:31:04.800 --> 01:31:06.900
As our nurses, training nurses

01:31:06.900 --> 01:31:11.031
and doctors go through
that initial education,

01:31:11.031 --> 01:31:14.670
really very good visit
and very informative.

01:31:14.670 --> 01:31:16.604
Might get David to comment a moment,

01:31:16.604 --> 01:31:18.330
but comments or questions, so Ruth?

01:31:18.330 --> 01:31:20.856
- Just to build to what Navina has said.

01:31:20.856 --> 01:31:22.622
Of course we did meet
the 50,000 nurse target

01:31:22.622 --> 01:31:26.730
six months early and as Navina has said,

01:31:26.730 --> 01:31:28.140
domestic supply, retention

01:31:28.140 --> 01:31:31.020
and of course international
recruitment part of it.

01:31:31.020 --> 01:31:33.150
So 93% of that target was achieved

01:31:33.150 --> 01:31:34.849
by international recruitment.

01:31:34.849 --> 01:31:36.189
- [Richard] I know, I know.

01:31:36.189 --> 01:31:37.500
- So it does show the need

01:31:37.500 --> 01:31:40.200
to have the long-term plan
so that we can rebalance

01:31:40.200 --> 01:31:43.391
domestic and international supply, yep.

01:31:43.391 --> 01:31:46.552
- Very good, other comments, yes.

01:31:46.552 --> 01:31:51.470
- Is it worth just commenting
a little bit, Navina,

01:31:51.470 --> 01:31:52.890
on the university partners obviously

01:31:52.890 --> 01:31:54.190
very key delivery partners

01:31:55.161 --> 01:31:57.035
in this, there's a lot
of noise in the press.

01:31:57.035 --> 01:31:58.341
- [Navina] Yes.

01:31:58.341 --> 01:31:59.649
- I'm entirely sure you're on it

01:31:59.649 --> 01:32:00.482
and we'd just love to hear more.

01:32:01.603 --> 01:32:04.510
- Yeah, so we work very
closely with Universities UK,

01:32:04.510 --> 01:32:05.959
office of students,

01:32:05.959 --> 01:32:07.380
Medical School Council, Council of Deans,

01:32:07.380 --> 01:32:10.290
and then at the regional
and ICB local level,

01:32:10.290 --> 01:32:12.990
they're really strong
partnerships between our trusts

01:32:12.990 --> 01:32:17.790
as placement providers and
higher and further education.

01:32:17.790 --> 01:32:20.780
So at all of those levels,
we've got a long history

01:32:20.780 --> 01:32:24.060
of strong working relationships.

01:32:24.060 --> 01:32:25.140
We also have a long history

01:32:25.140 --> 01:32:28.350
of solving problems when
they arise and they do.

01:32:28.350 --> 01:32:31.563
And I think it's really important
that we don't forget that,

01:32:32.899 --> 01:32:34.562
they give us feedback
about placements in the NHS

01:32:34.562 --> 01:32:36.360
when we have to tackle them.

01:32:36.360 --> 01:32:38.980
And we work with them
when there are concerns

01:32:39.846 --> 01:32:41.817
about the education aspects.

01:32:41.817 --> 01:32:44.460
They're all, I could say they,

01:32:44.460 --> 01:32:46.980
many of them were at the
summit and we continue

01:32:46.980 --> 01:32:48.420
to work with them.

01:32:48.420 --> 01:32:51.690
They are really keen to
be part of this expansion

01:32:51.690 --> 01:32:53.880
of placement for 2025.

01:32:53.880 --> 01:32:56.760
They are really keen to be part
of the delivery of the plan.

01:32:56.760 --> 01:32:59.670
They did work with us on
the development of the plan.

01:32:59.670 --> 01:33:03.360
There are of course
concerns about the capacity,

01:33:03.360 --> 01:33:07.320
the educator workforce,
which is our concern,

01:33:07.320 --> 01:33:10.350
but it's also theirs in
the education sector,

01:33:10.350 --> 01:33:15.060
the infrastructure, their ability

01:33:15.060 --> 01:33:19.795
to reform in terms of
changes to their curriculum.

01:33:19.795 --> 01:33:22.133
So we've got a number of work streams,

01:33:22.133 --> 01:33:24.930
which we work closely with them.

01:33:24.930 --> 01:33:28.320
The final thing to say is
through the ministerial board,

01:33:28.320 --> 01:33:30.960
which we are part of together
with cross government,

01:33:30.960 --> 01:33:33.074
which includes the
department for education

01:33:33.074 --> 01:33:34.800
and the treasury on that board.

01:33:34.800 --> 01:33:38.013
So we try to identify and
address issues that way.

01:33:38.945 --> 01:33:40.250
- Yeah, we're certainly,

01:33:40.250 --> 01:33:41.982
I mean you're working very
closely with government I think,

01:33:41.982 --> 01:33:43.320
which is really, really helpful

01:33:43.320 --> 01:33:45.360
across the relevant departments.

01:33:45.360 --> 01:33:48.060
And then Mary's made points earlier

01:33:48.060 --> 01:33:50.790
and heard correctly
about just making sure.

01:33:50.790 --> 01:33:53.460
We also think about, there's
not so much scaling up

01:33:53.460 --> 01:33:56.310
the number of doctors
and nurses to do that.

01:33:56.310 --> 01:33:58.710
We've got to scale up
the number of educators

01:33:58.710 --> 01:34:00.930
who are going to actually provide them

01:34:00.930 --> 01:34:02.643
with that training and oversight.

01:34:02.643 --> 01:34:04.980
- So we look at our
own educator workforce,

01:34:04.980 --> 01:34:07.590
but we work very closely
with them and together,

01:34:07.590 --> 01:34:09.660
raising the need for
their educator workforce

01:34:09.660 --> 01:34:12.000
to be expansion as well.

01:34:12.000 --> 01:34:14.160
- Now David, do you want
to comment on our trip

01:34:14.160 --> 01:34:16.040
to South Cumbria.

01:34:16.040 --> 01:34:17.280
It was good, it was really good,

01:34:17.280 --> 01:34:20.340
I think really informative, instructive.

01:34:20.340 --> 01:34:25.340
- So Richard and I went up
to the northwest of England

01:34:26.340 --> 01:34:31.340
and we spent some time
with the senior team

01:34:32.760 --> 01:34:35.940
and the people team at
Lancashire Teaching Hospital.

01:34:35.940 --> 01:34:37.260
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:34:37.260 --> 01:34:40.650
- We then met the system chief executives

01:34:40.650 --> 01:34:43.290
for dinner and spoke to
them about their approach

01:34:43.290 --> 01:34:44.640
to workforce planning.

01:34:44.640 --> 01:34:46.050
So that was the acute community

01:34:46.050 --> 01:34:49.380
and mental health trust chief executives.

01:34:49.380 --> 01:34:52.350
We then spent some time
with the ICB leadership

01:34:52.350 --> 01:34:54.302
about how they were approaching

01:34:54.302 --> 01:34:59.160
all the challenges, but
particularly the challenge

01:34:59.160 --> 01:35:00.930
around workforce development.

01:35:00.930 --> 01:35:04.770
And then the last part of our visit

01:35:04.770 --> 01:35:08.640
was to UCLAN, the University
of Central Lancashire.

01:35:08.640 --> 01:35:10.816
The purpose of going to that visit

01:35:10.816 --> 01:35:12.090
is UCLAN have been pretty innovative

01:35:12.090 --> 01:35:13.590
about their nurse education

01:35:13.590 --> 01:35:15.690
and their medical education.

01:35:15.690 --> 01:35:18.480
Particularly, so this is
the community I was born

01:35:18.480 --> 01:35:23.310
and brought up in, how in those industrial

01:35:23.310 --> 01:35:24.600
textile communities

01:35:24.600 --> 01:35:27.540
of the northwest have not
really had replacement industry

01:35:27.540 --> 01:35:29.235
since the eighties.

01:35:29.235 --> 01:35:33.900
Does UCLAN provide an offer
which provides different

01:35:33.900 --> 01:35:36.030
and alternative routes into education

01:35:36.030 --> 01:35:37.740
for people in those communities

01:35:37.740 --> 01:35:40.057
to enter the healthcare workforce

01:35:40.057 --> 01:35:41.776
or apprenticeship route,

01:35:41.776 --> 01:35:46.776
arrangements with both
Lancaster Teaching Hospital,

01:35:48.030 --> 01:35:51.060
the University at Lancaster,
but also some of the,

01:35:51.060 --> 01:35:55.523
and the local trust on how
they were providing curricula

01:35:55.523 --> 01:35:58.939
for operating theatre assistants,

01:35:58.939 --> 01:36:01.893
nurse associates, et cetera.

01:36:03.330 --> 01:36:04.230
And interestingly.

01:36:05.241 --> 01:36:06.330
- [Richard] Dentistry school.

01:36:06.330 --> 01:36:08.250
- A number of the operating
theatre assistants

01:36:08.250 --> 01:36:10.650
for people that had had
other assistant roles.

01:36:10.650 --> 01:36:13.600
And so this as a career
development, which was interesting.

01:36:14.790 --> 01:36:19.790
So I thought it provided a
really fascinating picture

01:36:19.920 --> 01:36:24.003
of the connections between the employers,

01:36:25.200 --> 01:36:28.743
the commissioners and the
providers of education.

01:36:29.820 --> 01:36:32.280
And I came away with a real sense

01:36:32.280 --> 01:36:35.970
of both what they'd achieved
but also what the potential

01:36:35.970 --> 01:36:39.116
to achieve even more was
in those communities.

01:36:39.116 --> 01:36:41.820
To pick up on one of the
points that Navina was making,

01:36:41.820 --> 01:36:43.350
we know that part of the northwest

01:36:43.350 --> 01:36:45.340
is in inverted commas, 'under-doctored'

01:36:46.500 --> 01:36:51.390
and therefore the medical school there,

01:36:51.390 --> 01:36:55.950
which was largely developed
with self funders coming in.

01:36:55.950 --> 01:36:58.350
So recruitment from abroad

01:36:58.350 --> 01:37:03.350
is now being balanced
with domestic students

01:37:04.920 --> 01:37:08.133
going in as a result of the workforce.

01:37:09.690 --> 01:37:13.856
I think the ambition really is to see

01:37:13.856 --> 01:37:18.840
how does the ICB convene
both the employers

01:37:18.840 --> 01:37:20.670
and the providers of education

01:37:20.670 --> 01:37:23.730
to have an articulate conversation

01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:26.710
about what the future service provision is

01:37:27.720 --> 01:37:29.730
and what is the workforce that's required

01:37:29.730 --> 01:37:31.747
to work in those services?

01:37:31.747 --> 01:37:34.860
And then how can the
university begin to provide

01:37:34.860 --> 01:37:39.087
that alongside some of the
further education providers,

01:37:40.268 --> 01:37:42.533
the colleges, because the potential

01:37:42.533 --> 01:37:47.145
for partnership arrangements between,

01:37:47.145 --> 01:37:52.145
last thing I'd say, Richard,
thank you for the opportunity

01:37:52.890 --> 01:37:56.430
is when I've been visiting universities,

01:37:56.430 --> 01:37:57.900
they've always said, what
will the health service

01:37:57.900 --> 01:38:00.510
look like in 15 years time and et cetera.

01:38:00.510 --> 01:38:02.790
And it's also an opportunity for us to say

01:38:02.790 --> 01:38:04.350
to the universities,

01:38:04.350 --> 01:38:07.056
what will the university
look like in 15 years time?

01:38:07.056 --> 01:38:10.950
So we've just had a conversation
about AI, technology,

01:38:10.950 --> 01:38:14.400
digital and development and
actually being transformative

01:38:14.400 --> 01:38:16.170
and it will be equally transformative

01:38:16.170 --> 01:38:18.750
of the way people learn in the future.

01:38:18.750 --> 01:38:21.540
So I think these issues
about blended learning

01:38:21.540 --> 01:38:24.600
need to be really featured in

01:38:24.600 --> 01:38:28.950
as part of the agenda of what is education

01:38:28.950 --> 01:38:33.081
going to look like as we go
forward and how do we then blend

01:38:33.081 --> 01:38:37.260
practise placements with
the way that education is?

01:38:37.260 --> 01:38:39.173
- And you and I saw we were showing

01:38:39.173 --> 01:38:42.900
simulation in terms of
training of patients.

01:38:42.900 --> 01:38:44.375
It's brilliant.

01:38:44.375 --> 01:38:45.692
- Very sophisticated.

01:38:45.692 --> 01:38:47.002
- [Richard] Very sophisticated.

01:38:47.002 --> 01:38:49.590
- £200,000 mannequins that
can pretty much do anything

01:38:49.590 --> 01:38:51.672
that any of us could do.

01:38:51.672 --> 01:38:53.190
- [Richard] And better, better and faster.

01:38:53.190 --> 01:38:55.075
- Probably doesn't hurt as much.

01:38:55.075 --> 01:38:58.667
And then the last thing I'd say

01:38:58.667 --> 01:39:02.580
for the board is the workforce
training education committee

01:39:02.580 --> 01:39:07.170
of this board meets next
week where all the points

01:39:07.170 --> 01:39:08.670
that Navina has gone through

01:39:08.670 --> 01:39:10.500
that are referenced in the report

01:39:10.500 --> 01:39:14.580
will go through the detailed
programme documentation

01:39:14.580 --> 01:39:18.330
to hopefully give the
board some satisfaction

01:39:18.330 --> 01:39:19.800
that there are delivery plans

01:39:19.800 --> 01:39:22.770
that support all these
commitments that are made here.

01:39:22.770 --> 01:39:26.760
And said this in the
private meeting earlier.

01:39:26.760 --> 01:39:31.260
But any, any member of the
board is welcome to attend

01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:34.194
that meeting and make a
contribution to that work.

01:39:34.194 --> 01:39:37.113
It really is important

01:39:37.113 --> 01:39:39.690
that this is owned by the whole board.

01:39:39.690 --> 01:39:44.190
Just because of, yeah,

01:39:44.190 --> 01:39:45.570
well just because it's important.

01:39:45.570 --> 01:39:46.470
It's owned by the.

01:39:47.548 --> 01:39:48.381
- I mean you chair that committee

01:39:48.381 --> 01:39:53.381
really ably with the support
of and the team and so on.

01:39:53.489 --> 01:39:55.110
But this will be a regular item.

01:39:55.110 --> 01:39:59.520
It's such an important piece
of transformational reform

01:39:59.520 --> 01:40:01.560
to plan our long-term workforce

01:40:01.560 --> 01:40:03.720
and then to execute on the milestones.

01:40:03.720 --> 01:40:06.660
So it will come very regularly
to the board I think.

01:40:06.660 --> 01:40:09.480
Let's move on, should we go to Ming?

01:40:09.480 --> 01:40:12.265
It's federated data platform update

01:40:12.265 --> 01:40:15.540
and we did touch on it
in the private board,

01:40:15.540 --> 01:40:17.970
but if I can start with my congratulations

01:40:17.970 --> 01:40:19.470
not just to you, but to the whole team

01:40:19.470 --> 01:40:21.690
actually Ben and and others and Julian

01:40:21.690 --> 01:40:26.070
and others who've sort of
manage what was quite a lengthy

01:40:26.070 --> 01:40:28.560
and complex process actually
to get to the announcement

01:40:28.560 --> 01:40:30.480
of a few days ago now.

01:40:30.480 --> 01:40:33.540
And I think this is like the
long-term workforce plan.

01:40:33.540 --> 01:40:35.760
I think this, when we look
back in a few years time,

01:40:35.760 --> 01:40:38.700
this is a substantive
step forward for the NHS

01:40:38.700 --> 01:40:41.430
in terms of its management of
its technology and its data.

01:40:41.430 --> 01:40:43.680
But mean do you want to give us an update?

01:40:43.680 --> 01:40:46.380
- Thanks chair and want
to extend my thanks

01:40:46.380 --> 01:40:47.790
to Jackie's team as well.

01:40:47.790 --> 01:40:49.625
- [Richard] Yeah, sorry.

01:40:49.625 --> 01:40:50.933
- We've done a huge.

01:40:50.933 --> 01:40:52.252
- [Richard] Well done Jackie.

01:40:52.252 --> 01:40:53.549
- Commercial work that's a bit of a first.

01:40:53.549 --> 01:40:57.270
So really delighted to inform the board

01:40:57.270 --> 01:41:00.810
that we've awarded the
federated data platform

01:41:00.810 --> 01:41:04.470
and associated services
contract to a consortium

01:41:04.470 --> 01:41:08.270
led by Palantir, including Accenture,

01:41:08.270 --> 01:41:13.270
PWC, and Next CSU, which
is really important

01:41:13.920 --> 01:41:16.050
because it demonstrates the breadth

01:41:16.050 --> 01:41:19.080
that FDP and the
associated services cover.

01:41:19.080 --> 01:41:20.520
It's not just a data platform

01:41:20.520 --> 01:41:24.194
but actually how we
then deploy and adopt it

01:41:24.194 --> 01:41:27.226
and create new products off it.

01:41:27.226 --> 01:41:29.532
In parallel, we also procured

01:41:29.532 --> 01:41:32.220
and awarded the contract

01:41:32.220 --> 01:41:34.740
for the privacy enhancing technology

01:41:34.740 --> 01:41:38.970
and that's really important to
maintain privacy of the data

01:41:38.970 --> 01:41:41.730
and that was awarded to IQVIA as well.

01:41:41.730 --> 01:41:44.520
And we have a commitment to make sure

01:41:44.520 --> 01:41:48.810
that before we launch a new
federated data platform,

01:41:48.810 --> 01:41:51.747
we'll also integrate the PET technology

01:41:51.747 --> 01:41:56.215
so that all the data going into
the federated data platform

01:41:56.215 --> 01:41:57.996
will have passed through the privacy

01:41:57.996 --> 01:41:59.806
enhancing technology first.

01:41:59.806 --> 01:42:01.200
So those two things are a first,

01:42:01.200 --> 01:42:04.470
the privacy enhancing technology
will be a core service

01:42:04.470 --> 01:42:06.959
that anyone in the NHS can draw on.

01:42:06.959 --> 01:42:08.940
So it's not just linked to the FDP

01:42:08.940 --> 01:42:11.550
but will also help us standardise

01:42:11.550 --> 01:42:13.296
the way that we de-identify data

01:42:13.296 --> 01:42:15.360
and make it available.

01:42:15.360 --> 01:42:19.290
It also becomes the technology
to support data sharing

01:42:19.290 --> 01:42:23.100
as an information governance
tool, which will then provide

01:42:23.100 --> 01:42:25.920
an audit trail of what the purpose

01:42:25.920 --> 01:42:27.600
of the data's been used for,

01:42:27.600 --> 01:42:30.210
where it's gone and who's
actually accessed it.

01:42:30.210 --> 01:42:33.330
All really important
factors for the public trust

01:42:33.330 --> 01:42:36.030
which this programme has been really,

01:42:36.030 --> 01:42:38.494
has tried really hard to maintain.

01:42:38.494 --> 01:42:41.915
So I'll just point out that

01:42:41.915 --> 01:42:44.648
this has been an ongoing project.

01:42:44.648 --> 01:42:49.320
There are some myths out there
of what the programme is.

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:52.710
What we are really trying
to do is connect the NHS.

01:42:52.710 --> 01:42:55.570
None of the data sharing
arrangements are changing.

01:42:55.570 --> 01:42:57.480
There's been lots of questions

01:42:57.480 --> 01:43:00.877
around whether FDP will
include primary care data

01:43:00.877 --> 01:43:04.079
because the data sharing
arrangements are not changing.

01:43:04.079 --> 01:43:09.079
If a local system in an ICS
has data sharing agreements

01:43:09.090 --> 01:43:11.010
with their primary care
and they're working

01:43:11.010 --> 01:43:13.920
as a collaborative system
as we're intending them,

01:43:13.920 --> 01:43:15.720
then absolutely primary care data

01:43:15.720 --> 01:43:17.850
can go in. At a national level.

01:43:17.850 --> 01:43:20.820
We don't have a national
feed of primary care data.

01:43:20.820 --> 01:43:23.850
So in the national use
cases it's not appropriate

01:43:23.850 --> 01:43:25.140
in the national use cases,

01:43:25.140 --> 01:43:28.320
it's not appropriate for
us to see identifiable data

01:43:28.320 --> 01:43:31.320
and therefore the platform
will separate those uses.

01:43:31.320 --> 01:43:35.070
So just wanted to reinforce
that it is a piece

01:43:35.070 --> 01:43:37.200
of technology, it's a piece of software

01:43:37.200 --> 01:43:40.800
and really it's going to
help redesign processes

01:43:40.800 --> 01:43:42.342
and enable those processes.

01:43:42.342 --> 01:43:45.840
But in effect we still have
the governance processes

01:43:45.840 --> 01:43:48.330
through the previously
announcing technology,

01:43:48.330 --> 01:43:50.488
but also the local data
sharing arrangement.

01:43:50.488 --> 01:43:55.488
We're now in transition and mobilisation.

01:43:55.560 --> 01:43:58.980
So both the suppliers
are now talking together

01:43:58.980 --> 01:44:03.060
to make sure that their
platforms integrate.

01:44:03.060 --> 01:44:04.590
We are very confident

01:44:04.590 --> 01:44:06.903
that they've worked together
elsewhere in the world.

01:44:06.903 --> 01:44:10.020
So we're very confident
that will be the case.

01:44:10.020 --> 01:44:12.660
We also during the
procurement, procured a number

01:44:12.660 --> 01:44:16.090
of core capabilities,
which they're now Palantir

01:44:17.027 --> 01:44:19.227
and their suppliers are actually
supporting the build off

01:44:19.227 --> 01:44:21.428
and and transition of that.

01:44:21.428 --> 01:44:25.320
We have a six month transition period

01:44:25.320 --> 01:44:27.870
by which we'll make sure
everything's working

01:44:27.870 --> 01:44:30.819
before we roll out and
deploy new products.

01:44:30.819 --> 01:44:33.870
In order to bring the
public alongside with us,

01:44:33.870 --> 01:44:37.440
we've done extensive
engagement with the profession,

01:44:37.440 --> 01:44:41.553
as well as public bodies
such as National Voices,

01:44:42.480 --> 01:44:45.060
Patients Association, Royal Colleges,

01:44:45.060 --> 01:44:47.400
and I wanted to thank members of the board

01:44:47.400 --> 01:44:49.890
that really supported us in speaking to,

01:44:49.890 --> 01:44:51.180
particularly to the Royal Colleges

01:44:51.180 --> 01:44:54.150
and Chris's team in communications

01:44:54.150 --> 01:44:56.370
who were a great help with the media

01:44:56.370 --> 01:44:58.710
and central support.

01:44:58.710 --> 01:45:01.350
We have set up in our aim

01:45:01.350 --> 01:45:03.150
to be more transparent going forward,

01:45:03.150 --> 01:45:06.270
a check and challenge group
with external stakeholders

01:45:06.270 --> 01:45:11.100
and those stakeholders will
help us define and support

01:45:11.100 --> 01:45:15.900
and check the use cases we
want to take forward for FDP

01:45:15.900 --> 01:45:17.910
and make sure that
we're not doing anything

01:45:17.910 --> 01:45:22.230
that is not acceptable

01:45:22.230 --> 01:45:26.703
or is not in line with the
way that we want to work.

01:45:27.628 --> 01:45:29.370
And that check and challenge group

01:45:29.370 --> 01:45:31.443
is actually chaired by the national,

01:45:31.443 --> 01:45:33.720
the CEO of National Voices.

01:45:33.720 --> 01:45:37.233
So that's an independent advisory group.

01:45:38.160 --> 01:45:41.142
As part of the engagement we'll
continue to work with ICBs

01:45:41.142 --> 01:45:45.360
and also our regions and trusts.

01:45:45.360 --> 01:45:49.560
We have a target of adoption at trust

01:45:49.560 --> 01:45:51.840
so that we can actually make the best use

01:45:51.840 --> 01:45:53.434
of the applications

01:45:53.434 --> 01:45:55.440
that we've already developed
during the pilot sites.

01:45:55.440 --> 01:45:56.790
And as part of that transition,

01:45:56.790 --> 01:45:59.970
our expectation is all the
applications we've built

01:45:59.970 --> 01:46:02.549
so far in the pilots will
be available on the single

01:46:02.549 --> 01:46:07.500
instance that we deploy
to each trust and ICB.

01:46:07.500 --> 01:46:10.032
So there's quite considerable
work on how we do that.

01:46:10.032 --> 01:46:12.150
We're working through with incentives

01:46:12.150 --> 01:46:16.350
of how we can get better
deployment and better adoption

01:46:16.350 --> 01:46:19.650
and we've started lots
of regional workshops

01:46:19.650 --> 01:46:21.450
with our regional colleagues

01:46:21.450 --> 01:46:25.080
as well as ICB chief execs
in order to make them aware

01:46:25.080 --> 01:46:28.290
of the functionality but more
importantly what they need

01:46:28.290 --> 01:46:32.373
to do to get ready to accept
and deploy the software.

01:46:33.210 --> 01:46:35.113
I was going to leave it there Chair.

01:46:37.094 --> 01:46:40.552
- Mike, first all congratulations

01:46:40.552 --> 01:46:41.385
on getting it over the line.

01:46:41.385 --> 01:46:42.810
I think we'd all agree around this table.

01:46:42.810 --> 01:46:44.640
It's a fundamental building block

01:46:44.640 --> 01:46:47.640
for re-engineering the
service in the future

01:46:47.640 --> 01:46:50.610
and also congratulations on
providing the right level

01:46:50.610 --> 01:46:53.223
of reassurance in terms
of how we will look after

01:46:53.223 --> 01:46:56.763
and handle people's data in the future.

01:46:58.009 --> 01:46:59.910
I just wonder if you comment
on the corollary of that,

01:46:59.910 --> 01:47:03.687
which is, what is the impact
on our ability to care

01:47:03.687 --> 01:47:08.687
for people who choose to
opt out of data sharing?

01:47:09.060 --> 01:47:11.670
Because clearly that's a
potential issue in the future.

01:47:11.670 --> 01:47:13.170
So I'd just be interested
to hear your views

01:47:13.170 --> 01:47:15.510
on what that could mean
in terms of our ability

01:47:15.510 --> 01:47:17.700
to support them in the future.

01:47:17.700 --> 01:47:18.810
- As part of this work

01:47:18.810 --> 01:47:21.264
and as part of, sorry my voice is going,

01:47:21.264 --> 01:47:24.100
a part of the data strategy programme.

01:47:24.100 --> 01:47:29.100
We've started a large scale
patient engagement narrative

01:47:31.410 --> 01:47:34.200
and also lots of workshops, et cetera.

01:47:34.200 --> 01:47:36.756
So as part of that we
are actually checking in

01:47:36.756 --> 01:47:39.274
what the views of opt out

01:47:39.274 --> 01:47:43.590
with the joint data policy unit.

01:47:43.590 --> 01:47:46.063
We are also re-looking
at what does opt out mean

01:47:46.063 --> 01:47:48.750
and how do we really
make sure that's aligned

01:47:48.750 --> 01:47:50.711
and understood by patients.

01:47:50.711 --> 01:47:53.910
I would say that we are tracking ops out

01:47:53.910 --> 01:47:56.912
quite well and at the moment,

01:47:56.912 --> 01:48:00.390
we saw a slight peak when
the announcements was made

01:48:00.390 --> 01:48:02.250
when there was quite a lot of social media

01:48:02.250 --> 01:48:05.370
by Foxglove and other privacy campaigners.

01:48:05.370 --> 01:48:10.290
But the opt-out level has
not really materialised

01:48:10.290 --> 01:48:12.060
as much as we feared.

01:48:12.060 --> 01:48:14.100
We've also done quite a lot of work

01:48:14.100 --> 01:48:16.430
with our frequently asked questions

01:48:16.430 --> 01:48:18.480
so that they're public facing

01:48:18.480 --> 01:48:19.890
and have tested that

01:48:19.890 --> 01:48:21.840
with our external stakeholder groups so

01:48:21.840 --> 01:48:24.930
that they've helped us shape the messages

01:48:24.930 --> 01:48:26.910
so that people can actually understand

01:48:26.910 --> 01:48:28.560
that for direct care,

01:48:28.560 --> 01:48:31.364
it's not in their interest to
opt out, obviously they can,

01:48:31.364 --> 01:48:33.189
but it's unlikely.

01:48:33.189 --> 01:48:37.380
For secondary use, once we
explain what we want to do,

01:48:37.380 --> 01:48:39.960
patients are being very supportive

01:48:39.960 --> 01:48:43.503
and actually I think there's
a wider narrative around,

01:48:44.460 --> 01:48:46.770
you know, I was at our
future health the other day,

01:48:46.770 --> 01:48:48.780
people actually want to
contribute their health data

01:48:48.780 --> 01:48:50.850
for the NHS to do more research

01:48:50.850 --> 01:48:52.860
and actually to improve services.

01:48:52.860 --> 01:48:55.710
So I think there are quite
a lot of important voices

01:48:55.710 --> 01:48:58.650
that we need to raise as
part of that deliberative

01:48:58.650 --> 01:49:00.420
that we'll do with the public.

01:49:00.420 --> 01:49:03.510
And I think putting in place the controls

01:49:03.510 --> 01:49:06.660
and the transparency of
who's using your data

01:49:06.660 --> 01:49:08.747
for what purpose will support that.

01:49:08.747 --> 01:49:12.930
We are doing some work with
a couple of organisations

01:49:12.930 --> 01:49:17.340
to look at what a data donation
metaverse would look like

01:49:17.340 --> 01:49:20.427
so that we can actually
visualise it for the public

01:49:20.427 --> 01:49:23.010
to bring it to life.

01:49:23.010 --> 01:49:26.313
Otherwise it's quite a
difficult concept to grasp.

01:49:27.552 --> 01:49:31.882
- So I think we'll probably
touch on federated data platform

01:49:31.882 --> 01:49:33.238
in future meetings in the CEO's report

01:49:33.238 --> 01:49:36.750
and as we get substantial success

01:49:36.750 --> 01:49:39.120
or other or material challenge,

01:49:39.120 --> 01:49:41.970
otherwise I think we'll
continue to monitor it

01:49:41.970 --> 01:49:45.303
in the DDAT committee.

01:49:46.470 --> 01:49:49.590
But what I think from my
perspective is really important

01:49:49.590 --> 01:49:52.653
is that we incentivize the right take up

01:49:52.653 --> 01:49:56.670
from across all of the
trusts and all the systems.

01:49:56.670 --> 01:49:59.640
This is an ability to
synthesise nationally the data

01:49:59.640 --> 01:50:02.400
across England's trust secondary system.

01:50:02.400 --> 01:50:03.760
You can capture primary

01:50:04.960 --> 01:50:06.810
but it's a different type of interaction.

01:50:06.810 --> 01:50:08.760
So it's really important
we incentivize that

01:50:08.760 --> 01:50:11.340
and get the FDP to be that single

01:50:11.340 --> 01:50:13.593
national synthesised system actually,

01:50:14.430 --> 01:50:15.570
and for me that would be a really,

01:50:15.570 --> 01:50:17.190
really important step forward.

01:50:17.190 --> 01:50:18.023
David.

01:50:19.016 --> 01:50:21.817
- Thank you, Ming again, congratulations.

01:50:21.817 --> 01:50:25.110
I'm fascinated by
paragraph 11 in your report

01:50:25.110 --> 01:50:26.730
where you talk about the existing tools

01:50:26.730 --> 01:50:29.907
to focus on priority areas
such as care coordination,

01:50:29.907 --> 01:50:33.540
virtual wards, and optimising discharge.

01:50:33.540 --> 01:50:34.810
Richard, just to pick up on your point

01:50:34.810 --> 01:50:37.320
about looking forward, I
wonder whether we could

01:50:37.320 --> 01:50:39.630
have some of those stories.

01:50:39.630 --> 01:50:42.660
'Cause in a sense it took me
ages to get what this meant

01:50:42.660 --> 01:50:46.080
and actually if we could just make it live

01:50:46.080 --> 01:50:48.300
about how those pilot trusts

01:50:48.300 --> 01:50:52.290
have optimised improvements
in discharge, for instance,

01:50:52.290 --> 01:50:54.870
or in care coordination,

01:50:54.870 --> 01:50:57.190
then I think it would
really punch its weight

01:50:58.050 --> 01:51:01.350
and as Mike said, be
the transformative tool

01:51:01.350 --> 01:51:03.387
that we want it to be.

01:51:03.387 --> 01:51:05.940
And I just wondered now
the procurement's done,

01:51:05.940 --> 01:51:10.410
I'd just really encourage
us to think about

01:51:10.410 --> 01:51:12.630
telling this through stories.

01:51:12.630 --> 01:51:15.540
of real life improvements that have.

01:51:15.540 --> 01:51:18.110
- We do on our website,

01:51:18.110 --> 01:51:21.510
we do have case studies from those sites

01:51:21.510 --> 01:51:22.972
that have already been piloting

01:51:22.972 --> 01:51:25.140
and there were some videos on there,

01:51:25.140 --> 01:51:26.970
but very happy to bring back to the board.

01:51:26.970 --> 01:51:29.683
- Yeah 'cause I think what it'll do is,

01:51:29.683 --> 01:51:31.650
I think the consent point
that Mike was exploring

01:51:31.650 --> 01:51:34.320
with you about confidentiality, et cetera,

01:51:34.320 --> 01:51:36.750
people saw that's the usage being put to,

01:51:36.750 --> 01:51:39.030
I think get a bigger buy.

01:51:39.030 --> 01:51:40.380
- [Ming] Yes.

01:51:40.380 --> 01:51:42.140
- Very good, thank you
and Jackie, thank you.

01:51:42.140 --> 01:51:44.840
I forgot thank you to you and your team.

01:51:44.840 --> 01:51:47.970
As I did observe the
intensity of the discussions

01:51:47.970 --> 01:51:48.810
and the negotiations

01:51:48.810 --> 01:51:53.430
both with third party
consortium providers,

01:51:53.430 --> 01:51:55.380
but also working it through

01:51:55.380 --> 01:51:58.166
the various government
departments where we need

01:51:58.166 --> 01:51:59.370
to clearly get support and approval.

01:51:59.370 --> 01:52:02.040
So thank you very much for all of that.

01:52:02.040 --> 01:52:03.690
Let's go on if we can to the last sort

01:52:03.690 --> 01:52:05.610
of substantive item for discussion.

01:52:05.610 --> 01:52:08.217
There is an item under governance

01:52:08.217 --> 01:52:10.050
and assurance, which is for noting,

01:52:10.050 --> 01:52:12.300
but John Stewart welcome,

01:52:12.300 --> 01:52:14.050
I think John's going
to lead Julian on this,

01:52:14.050 --> 01:52:16.441
I think is right.

01:52:16.441 --> 01:52:19.980
So director of specialised
commissioning John Stewart.

01:52:19.980 --> 01:52:21.930
Next steps with regard to delegation down

01:52:21.930 --> 01:52:24.390
to the integrated care board, the ICBs,

01:52:24.390 --> 01:52:25.293
- Thank you chair.

01:52:26.280 --> 01:52:29.190
Right, so the board has
previously been cited

01:52:29.190 --> 01:52:30.570
and supportive of our plans

01:52:30.570 --> 01:52:32.400
for delegating
commissioning responsibility

01:52:32.400 --> 01:52:35.351
for suitable specialised services
to integrated care boards,

01:52:35.351 --> 01:52:37.710
really in order to support

01:52:37.710 --> 01:52:40.775
both a population health
management approach

01:52:40.775 --> 01:52:42.090
across whole pathways of care

01:52:42.090 --> 01:52:45.270
and to help tackle health
inequalities in terms of access

01:52:45.270 --> 01:52:49.620
to quality of and outcomes
from specialised services.

01:52:49.620 --> 01:52:51.330
Now, just as a reminder,

01:52:51.330 --> 01:52:53.820
NHS England is the
accountable commissioner

01:52:53.820 --> 01:52:56.460
for a diverse portfolio of 150,

01:52:56.460 --> 01:52:59.280
roughly 150 specialised services.

01:52:59.280 --> 01:53:02.760
And over the last decade we
have taken important steps

01:53:02.760 --> 01:53:06.240
to improve quality and address
variation through the setting

01:53:06.240 --> 01:53:08.490
of consistent national standards.

01:53:08.490 --> 01:53:10.110
And through the development

01:53:10.110 --> 01:53:12.720
of national clinical access policies,

01:53:12.720 --> 01:53:14.790
our role as the accountable commissioner

01:53:14.790 --> 01:53:17.250
for these services is not going to change.

01:53:17.250 --> 01:53:20.220
And NHS England will
continue to be responsible

01:53:20.220 --> 01:53:21.840
for setting a national framework

01:53:21.840 --> 01:53:24.900
of mandatory standards,
service specifications

01:53:24.900 --> 01:53:27.563
and clinical access
policies as we have done

01:53:27.563 --> 01:53:30.300
for the last 10 years.

01:53:30.300 --> 01:53:33.300
But the creation of
integrated care boards,

01:53:33.300 --> 01:53:36.690
which operate now on a
much larger footprint

01:53:36.690 --> 01:53:39.930
to the old CCGs combined with new powers

01:53:39.930 --> 01:53:43.800
in the Health and Care Act from 2022,

01:53:43.800 --> 01:53:45.900
do provide an important opportunity now

01:53:45.900 --> 01:53:47.220
to address fragmented

01:53:47.220 --> 01:53:50.100
and siloed commissioning
arrangements that have arisen

01:53:50.100 --> 01:53:53.310
as a result of often
quite artificial splits

01:53:53.310 --> 01:53:55.380
in commissioning responsibility

01:53:55.380 --> 01:53:57.450
between NHS England and ICBs.

01:53:57.450 --> 01:53:59.940
And maybe I can just
kind of illustrate that

01:53:59.940 --> 01:54:01.740
a bit with a few examples.

01:54:01.740 --> 01:54:05.940
So if you take adult
critical care to start with,

01:54:05.940 --> 01:54:07.200
so roughly 50%

01:54:07.200 --> 01:54:10.350
of adult critical care
activity is commissioned

01:54:10.350 --> 01:54:12.450
and paid for by NHS England.

01:54:12.450 --> 01:54:15.900
And that relates to services
where placements relate

01:54:15.900 --> 01:54:17.850
to a specialised service.

01:54:17.850 --> 01:54:21.720
The other 50% is commissioned
and paid for by ICBs.

01:54:21.720 --> 01:54:25.230
That doesn't lead to collective,

01:54:25.230 --> 01:54:27.540
joint planning terribly easily.

01:54:27.540 --> 01:54:29.389
If you take renal services,

01:54:29.389 --> 01:54:33.630
ICBs are responsible for
commissioning primary

01:54:33.630 --> 01:54:35.010
and secondary care services

01:54:35.010 --> 01:54:37.650
for patients with chronic kidney disease.

01:54:37.650 --> 01:54:39.450
But NHS England is responsible

01:54:39.450 --> 01:54:41.460
for commissioning renal
replacement therapy.

01:54:41.460 --> 01:54:46.460
So renal dialysis, so again,
a kind of slightly odd split.

01:54:46.981 --> 01:54:50.070
Maternity services and neonatal services,

01:54:50.070 --> 01:54:52.050
which are inextricably linked.

01:54:52.050 --> 01:54:55.590
ICBs commission maternity
services, NHS England commissions,

01:54:55.590 --> 01:54:59.473
neonatal services, mental health services,

01:54:59.473 --> 01:55:02.100
inpatient mental health services,

01:55:02.100 --> 01:55:03.690
learning disability services

01:55:03.690 --> 01:55:07.786
and autism services,
NHS England Commissions,

01:55:07.786 --> 01:55:09.330
the inpatient specialist care,

01:55:09.330 --> 01:55:11.910
ICBs Commission Community Services.

01:55:11.910 --> 01:55:16.910
Maybe finally as an example,
neurology. NHS England

01:55:17.040 --> 01:55:18.630
pays for anything that happens

01:55:18.630 --> 01:55:21.300
to take place in the neurosciences centre.

01:55:21.300 --> 01:55:26.300
Whereas ICBs pay for anything
that takes place in a DGH.

01:55:26.730 --> 01:55:29.130
So I think hopefully that illustrates

01:55:29.130 --> 01:55:31.830
some of the slightly fragmented

01:55:31.830 --> 01:55:33.990
commissioning responsibilities we've got.

01:55:33.990 --> 01:55:36.150
So back in February the
board approved plans

01:55:36.150 --> 01:55:38.400
to delegate commissioning responsibility,

01:55:38.400 --> 01:55:39.990
not for the whole portfolio

01:55:39.990 --> 01:55:42.840
but for 59 specialised services

01:55:42.840 --> 01:55:44.880
to nine statutory joint committees

01:55:44.880 --> 01:55:46.860
formed between integrated care boards

01:55:46.860 --> 01:55:50.070
and our regional NHS England teams.

01:55:50.070 --> 01:55:51.930
And that was really as a transitional step

01:55:51.930 --> 01:55:53.940
towards delegating full responsibility

01:55:53.940 --> 01:55:55.590
for suitable services

01:55:55.590 --> 01:56:00.360
to ICBs from 24 onwards, April 24 onwards.

01:56:00.360 --> 01:56:02.610
So we're now coming back to the board

01:56:02.610 --> 01:56:05.190
to agree our plans for 24, 25

01:56:05.190 --> 01:56:08.310
and in summary, we are
proposing to move forward

01:56:08.310 --> 01:56:10.980
with delegating full
commissioning responsibility

01:56:10.980 --> 01:56:13.050
for 59 services.

01:56:13.050 --> 01:56:16.080
So the same set of services
that have been subject

01:56:16.080 --> 01:56:18.840
of joint commissioning
arrangements this year

01:56:18.840 --> 01:56:22.230
to ICBs across the east
of England, Midlands,

01:56:22.230 --> 01:56:24.570
and the northwest regions.

01:56:24.570 --> 01:56:27.300
And to maintain joint
commissioning arrangements

01:56:27.300 --> 01:56:30.720
just for one final year
with ICBs across London,

01:56:30.720 --> 01:56:34.620
Southeast, Southwest, Northeast
and Yorkshire regions.

01:56:34.620 --> 01:56:38.910
And that reflects, I
think, a different level

01:56:38.910 --> 01:56:41.280
of confidence amongst those ICBs

01:56:41.280 --> 01:56:44.850
and regions in pressing
ahead with delegation.

01:56:44.850 --> 01:56:47.640
So we want to support those regions

01:56:47.640 --> 01:56:48.870
and ICBs that are feeling confident

01:56:48.870 --> 01:56:51.141
and want to really progress
and see the benefits.

01:56:51.141 --> 01:56:56.141
And we think it's absolutely
right to get those other areas

01:56:56.820 --> 01:56:58.320
a little bit more time to make sure

01:56:58.320 --> 01:57:01.320
that we see a smooth transition.

01:57:01.320 --> 01:57:04.470
So just a few final points.

01:57:04.470 --> 01:57:08.400
These recommendations I think
are consistent with kind

01:57:08.400 --> 01:57:11.160
of a careful and considered
approach for board,

01:57:11.160 --> 01:57:13.620
I think encouraged us
to take in this area.

01:57:13.620 --> 01:57:15.210
I think they could see the benefit,

01:57:15.210 --> 01:57:18.540
but recognised that this
isn't an insignificant

01:57:18.540 --> 01:57:21.010
shift in responsibility.

01:57:21.010 --> 01:57:24.180
And all of these decisions
have been informed

01:57:24.180 --> 01:57:27.571
by really thorough assessment
of system readiness

01:57:27.571 --> 01:57:30.690
that have been conducted jointly
between our regional teams

01:57:30.690 --> 01:57:33.870
and in partnership with
our integrated care boards.

01:57:33.870 --> 01:57:36.270
Finally, as we prepare for implementation,

01:57:36.270 --> 01:57:38.520
I think it's just worth
highlighting four areas

01:57:38.520 --> 01:57:39.690
in particular

01:57:39.690 --> 01:57:42.600
where we are paying
particularly close attention.

01:57:42.600 --> 01:57:45.810
Firstly, ensuring our expert
commissioning capacity

01:57:45.810 --> 01:57:47.370
and capability is retained

01:57:47.370 --> 01:57:49.950
within the system as
we make these changes.

01:57:49.950 --> 01:57:52.860
Secondly, making sure the
new financial architecture

01:57:52.860 --> 01:57:54.930
and financial flows don't lead

01:57:54.930 --> 01:57:56.970
to any loss in financial control

01:57:56.970 --> 01:57:58.290
and that there are no sudden

01:57:58.290 --> 01:58:01.350
or destabilising impacts on providers.

01:58:01.350 --> 01:58:03.870
Thirdly, making sure
that the necessary data

01:58:03.870 --> 01:58:06.090
and business intelligence requirements

01:58:06.090 --> 01:58:08.640
to support effective
commissioning are in place.

01:58:08.640 --> 01:58:11.730
And lastly that we do
have robust oversight

01:58:11.730 --> 01:58:13.860
and assurance mechanisms in place

01:58:13.860 --> 01:58:16.473
for those services we are delegating.

01:58:19.194 --> 01:58:20.869
Thank you, I mean, very clear, one,

01:58:20.869 --> 01:58:22.976
the paper was very clear and very good.

01:58:22.976 --> 01:58:24.807
And secondly, that
presentation was excellent.

01:58:24.807 --> 01:58:26.103
Thank you very much of the key issues.

01:58:26.103 --> 01:58:29.883
So reactions or comments, questions,

01:58:31.740 --> 01:58:34.326
David and then Mary.

01:58:34.326 --> 01:58:35.820
Sorry, David first, then Mary.

01:58:35.820 --> 01:58:39.879
- Thanks John, I agree with
Richard about it being clear.

01:58:39.879 --> 01:58:42.996
I was trying to work out
though the convergence policy

01:58:42.996 --> 01:58:45.480
and what that meant
and I couldn't work out

01:58:45.480 --> 01:58:48.313
whether a lot of those
that were showing plus

01:58:48.313 --> 01:58:53.313
were actually the places with
big specialist centres in them

01:58:54.330 --> 01:58:56.890
and the ones that were minus

01:58:58.020 --> 01:58:59.613
were the places without them.

01:59:00.464 --> 01:59:03.060
And I was, it just made me
think about the practicalities

01:59:03.060 --> 01:59:04.740
of how that was going to work.

01:59:04.740 --> 01:59:07.293
- You want me to hold your answer

01:59:08.167 --> 01:59:10.050
until we get to Mary.

01:59:10.050 --> 01:59:12.330
- It makes a huge amount of sense to me

01:59:12.330 --> 01:59:14.490
and I think it's very well explained.

01:59:14.490 --> 01:59:16.830
I just wondered whether
you could give us a flavour

01:59:16.830 --> 01:59:20.313
of anything that's going
to be kept at the centre.

01:59:21.540 --> 01:59:25.440
- And then the question
I have is without naming

01:59:25.440 --> 01:59:27.420
the tertiary specialist hospital,

01:59:27.420 --> 01:59:30.780
you and I met yesterday
afternoon with one of the trusts

01:59:30.780 --> 01:59:33.180
and one of the points they made actually

01:59:33.180 --> 01:59:37.650
after you stepped out was
just a question about whether,

01:59:37.650 --> 01:59:39.565
I'm not sure how it would work,

01:59:39.565 --> 01:59:42.210
but whether there's a
de-minimis protection

01:59:42.210 --> 01:59:43.950
in terms of services commission

01:59:43.950 --> 01:59:48.000
that they would specialise in
on the grounds that as you,

01:59:48.000 --> 01:59:50.730
'cause it was a category rather
than a particular condition,

01:59:50.730 --> 01:59:52.713
a category of population.

01:59:53.710 --> 01:59:56.190
Is there a danger, was there question

01:59:56.190 --> 01:59:59.670
that as we delegate these responsibilities

01:59:59.670 --> 02:00:01.628
standard to the ICBs

02:00:01.628 --> 02:00:02.910
that the pressure, the budget the ICBs

02:00:02.910 --> 02:00:04.560
'cause we're essentially dividing

02:00:04.560 --> 02:00:07.500
what might have been centrally
a specialised commissioning

02:00:07.500 --> 02:00:10.893
is splits essentially into into 42.

02:00:12.395 --> 02:00:15.086
Under budget pressure,

02:00:15.086 --> 02:00:17.200
do they simply let some of those smaller

02:00:18.538 --> 02:00:21.150
cost items against the over
budget fall off the table?

02:00:21.150 --> 02:00:23.130
And it's just a question of how,

02:00:23.130 --> 02:00:24.480
I probably haven't explained it very well,

02:00:24.480 --> 02:00:27.130
but how well do we protect against

02:00:28.328 --> 02:00:31.150
that we're an aggregate at
the centre for a population

02:00:32.371 --> 02:00:35.580
of a particular cohort of the population.

02:00:35.580 --> 02:00:37.230
Actually it was quite
clear that you could see

02:00:37.230 --> 02:00:40.140
from a central basis
commissioning enough resource

02:00:40.140 --> 02:00:44.430
but divided by 42 does
it actually get through?

02:00:44.430 --> 02:00:46.560
And they were concerned that
actually without some sort

02:00:46.560 --> 02:00:50.040
of de-minimis protection
pressures on commissioning budgets

02:00:50.040 --> 02:00:54.630
in ICBs might damage the financial support

02:00:54.630 --> 02:00:57.363
to commissioning for
that cohort of patients.

02:00:58.200 --> 02:01:00.180
Long-winded rubbish question.

02:01:00.180 --> 02:01:02.100
Not very able to explain question.

02:01:02.100 --> 02:01:04.290
- Say let me try and take them in order.

02:01:04.290 --> 02:01:09.290
So firstly David's point
about distance from target.

02:01:09.780 --> 02:01:11.520
So actually what I didn't mention

02:01:11.520 --> 02:01:14.760
is to support all of these
changes, we are moving now

02:01:14.760 --> 02:01:18.900
and have moved this year to
a population based approach

02:01:18.900 --> 02:01:20.610
to funding specialised services

02:01:20.610 --> 02:01:25.200
and away from the historic
approach which has been based

02:01:25.200 --> 02:01:29.310
on a provider basis on
historic activity levels.

02:01:29.310 --> 02:01:32.610
That is a really, really
important step for us

02:01:32.610 --> 02:01:35.160
to take in terms of actually
supporting health inequalities

02:01:35.160 --> 02:01:37.500
and potentially the most
important thing structurally

02:01:37.500 --> 02:01:39.750
I think we can do in specialised services

02:01:39.750 --> 02:01:42.630
to support that, there
will probably be all sorts

02:01:42.630 --> 02:01:45.840
of different reasons why
some are below and high.

02:01:45.840 --> 02:01:48.826
And I think it's absolutely
the case that we have seen

02:01:48.826 --> 02:01:51.330
those patients who
happen to be living close

02:01:51.330 --> 02:01:56.280
to a specialised hospital
might tend to get better access

02:01:56.280 --> 02:01:58.260
to those services.

02:01:58.260 --> 02:02:01.283
And I think this now begins
to let us look at all of this.

02:02:01.283 --> 02:02:04.290
So what we've set out in
that annex is, actually,

02:02:04.290 --> 02:02:09.290
how far from overall or
above, under or above target,

02:02:09.480 --> 02:02:11.400
ICBs are, when you look at

02:02:11.400 --> 02:02:13.590
that funding on a population basis

02:02:13.590 --> 02:02:15.870
and then we will need to very carefully

02:02:15.870 --> 02:02:20.870
look at how we begin to move
those ICBs towards target

02:02:21.060 --> 02:02:24.075
over the course of,
probably quite a number

02:02:24.075 --> 02:02:27.120
of years and that will
be heavily dependent

02:02:27.120 --> 02:02:31.590
on future government funding decisions.

02:02:31.590 --> 02:02:34.560
So we will be really
cautious in that approach

02:02:34.560 --> 02:02:37.914
and I think I can say for
next year that you know,

02:02:37.914 --> 02:02:39.510
we would expect every ICB to get

02:02:39.510 --> 02:02:41.340
a minimum level of growth.

02:02:41.340 --> 02:02:44.965
So I think that's important to say.

02:02:44.965 --> 02:02:47.010
Second point about what's kept,

02:02:47.010 --> 02:02:49.320
well actually quite a lot is kept,

02:02:49.320 --> 02:02:52.860
so for portfolio 150
services, if my math is right,

02:02:52.860 --> 02:02:56.280
that's about 80 services that are left,

02:02:56.280 --> 02:02:58.080
most of those are what we refer to

02:02:58.080 --> 02:02:59.970
as highly specialised services

02:02:59.970 --> 02:03:03.394
and they tend to be services
that we are commissioning

02:03:03.394 --> 02:03:06.690
for a very small number of patients.

02:03:06.690 --> 02:03:10.110
Often kind of around 500 patients,

02:03:10.110 --> 02:03:12.747
often a very small number of centres.

02:03:12.747 --> 02:03:14.940
Sometimes, you know, it might only be one

02:03:14.940 --> 02:03:17.070
or two centres across the country.

02:03:17.070 --> 02:03:19.020
And we recognise that the benefits

02:03:19.020 --> 02:03:21.330
of delegating those are much more limited.

02:03:21.330 --> 02:03:23.160
So they will all be retained

02:03:23.160 --> 02:03:26.190
and commissioned essentially
by NHS England nationally.

02:03:26.190 --> 02:03:27.870
There is a small set of services,

02:03:27.870 --> 02:03:31.110
about 30 or so, that we are still yet

02:03:31.110 --> 02:03:34.140
to take a final view on
whether they should stay

02:03:34.140 --> 02:03:36.510
with NHS England or be delegated

02:03:36.510 --> 02:03:38.820
and we will make sure that
we've taken final decisions

02:03:38.820 --> 02:03:43.820
on that by April 25, which is
the point where we will look

02:03:44.010 --> 02:03:48.540
to delegate across the country
the remaining services.

02:03:48.540 --> 02:03:49.630
And then Richard, finally.

02:03:49.630 --> 02:03:51.600
- [Richard] You understand my question?

02:03:51.600 --> 02:03:55.430
- Well I'll provide an answer.

02:03:58.800 --> 02:03:59.850
- To the question you
think I should have asked.

02:03:59.850 --> 02:04:03.750
- So I think people
would rightly be worried

02:04:03.750 --> 02:04:08.550
if we were moving to arrangement
whereby our providers

02:04:08.550 --> 02:04:12.480
were ending up having to have
relationships with 42 ICBs

02:04:12.480 --> 02:04:15.900
and that's absolutely not
what we're planning at all.

02:04:15.900 --> 02:04:19.470
So, and that's why the
decision the board took

02:04:19.470 --> 02:04:21.540
around joint committees this year,

02:04:21.540 --> 02:04:25.080
nine joint committees, I
think is really important.

02:04:25.080 --> 02:04:29.133
'Cause what we will see is
effectively nine geographies

02:04:29.133 --> 02:04:31.830
where ICBs come together

02:04:31.830 --> 02:04:34.410
to commission those services jointly,

02:04:34.410 --> 02:04:36.750
share pool risk and so on.

02:04:36.750 --> 02:04:38.991
And that will avoid a situation

02:04:38.991 --> 02:04:40.800
where some providers are
going to have to have

02:04:40.800 --> 02:04:44.190
kind of contractual relationships
with 42 different areas.

02:04:44.190 --> 02:04:46.140
So I think that's really important.

02:04:46.140 --> 02:04:50.578
It varies around the country
depending on the size of ICBs.

02:04:50.578 --> 02:04:54.630
But in terms of the
areas that we are looking

02:04:54.630 --> 02:04:58.863
to delegate from April
next year, in the Midlands,

02:04:58.863 --> 02:05:01.410
we're actually looking
at a east west split.

02:05:01.410 --> 02:05:05.460
So those services will be
commissioned on that basis,

02:05:05.460 --> 02:05:09.570
in the northwest because
of the size of our ICBs,

02:05:09.570 --> 02:05:11.790
they have actually done a
lot of really careful work

02:05:11.790 --> 02:05:13.410
to look at which of us are services

02:05:13.410 --> 02:05:17.063
in that portfolio of 59 can be delegated

02:05:17.063 --> 02:05:20.430
and organised at an individual ICB level,

02:05:20.430 --> 02:05:25.080
but which ones need to kind
of have a pan ICB approach.

02:05:25.080 --> 02:05:27.570
So slightly different
timber, the northwest

02:05:27.570 --> 02:05:29.040
and then in the east of England.

02:05:29.040 --> 02:05:31.457
I think all the ICBs are coming together,

02:05:31.457 --> 02:05:33.990
as per this year into a kind of

02:05:33.990 --> 02:05:38.340
joint commissioning approach
with all of the areas

02:05:38.340 --> 02:05:41.880
being supported by our
current commissioning teams

02:05:41.880 --> 02:05:43.623
throughout 24 25.

02:05:45.240 --> 02:05:47.700
- Very good, any other
comments or questions?

02:05:47.700 --> 02:05:49.571
John, thank you, really clear.

02:05:49.571 --> 02:05:50.640
Thank you very, very much.

02:05:50.640 --> 02:05:54.060
Yeah, so if I just ask the board to note

02:05:54.060 --> 02:05:57.330
there's are directions
from the Secretary state

02:05:57.330 --> 02:05:59.460
on National Obesity Audit,

02:05:59.460 --> 02:06:01.590
which I'm asking the board just to note

02:06:01.590 --> 02:06:04.290
that was issued to us in November.

02:06:04.290 --> 02:06:07.050
Actually, just as a
sort of a side comment,

02:06:07.050 --> 02:06:09.870
I think it's 28% of the UK
adult population is obese.

02:06:09.870 --> 02:06:12.000
I think we are the most
obese nation in Europe.

02:06:12.000 --> 02:06:13.914
If I believe the newspapers.

02:06:13.914 --> 02:06:15.241
I don't always believe the newspapers,

02:06:15.241 --> 02:06:18.660
but on this occasion I
think I would believe that.

02:06:18.660 --> 02:06:22.890
And obesity causes, I
think the latest estimate,

02:06:22.890 --> 02:06:24.900
I think it was a Blair
Institute piece of research,

02:06:24.900 --> 02:06:26.820
I said it's 100 billion

02:06:26.820 --> 02:06:31.260
of which 19 billion is the
cost to the NHS and obesity.

02:06:31.260 --> 02:06:34.082
We know musculoskeletal
issues, cardiac issues,

02:06:34.082 --> 02:06:37.521
diabetes too, 13 cancers
are triggered by it.

02:06:37.521 --> 02:06:42.330
It's a very, very significant
problem in our society

02:06:42.330 --> 02:06:46.710
where a lot of the causes of
it lie outside are control.

02:06:46.710 --> 02:06:49.410
But I'm assuming the
board is happy to note

02:06:49.410 --> 02:06:52.833
the direction on national obesity audit.

02:06:54.180 --> 02:06:58.713
So can I go quickly round on,
is there any other business

02:06:58.713 --> 02:07:00.013
that people want to raise?

02:07:01.140 --> 02:07:03.271
In which case can I just then

02:07:03.271 --> 02:07:07.230
the next meeting is the
1st of February, 2024

02:07:09.330 --> 02:07:11.130
and it's going to actually take place

02:07:11.130 --> 02:07:12.900
in the east of England.

02:07:12.900 --> 02:07:14.777
I won't narrow it more down,

02:07:14.777 --> 02:07:16.481
I won't narrow it down more than that

02:07:16.481 --> 02:07:18.018
at the moment we're heading east.

02:07:18.018 --> 02:07:21.360
I would just like to reiterate
what I said at the start,

02:07:21.360 --> 02:07:23.640
which is just to again acknowledge

02:07:23.640 --> 02:07:28.080
and thank Susan Kilsby and
Rakesh Kapoor and Munir,

02:07:28.080 --> 02:07:32.430
thank you hugely actually
for an immense contribution

02:07:32.430 --> 02:07:34.110
to the work of the NHS England board

02:07:34.110 --> 02:07:35.827
and and to the NHS

02:07:35.827 --> 02:07:40.320
and you've been an absolutely
sterling colleague,

02:07:40.320 --> 02:07:41.864
so thank you.

02:07:41.864 --> 02:07:44.507
I was going to say sort of best.

02:07:44.507 --> 02:07:45.846
- Can I just add one thing to that?

02:07:45.846 --> 02:07:47.163
- You may.

02:07:47.163 --> 02:07:47.996
- Sorry just 'cause while
we're thanking people,

02:07:47.996 --> 02:07:50.657
'cause I think I've got
earlier and I'm really sorry

02:07:50.657 --> 02:07:52.860
but in welcoming Emily to the board,

02:07:52.860 --> 02:07:56.520
we should have just thanked
Jim Mackey who stepped in

02:07:56.520 --> 02:07:58.728
to cover the chief operating officer role

02:07:58.728 --> 02:08:03.360
from David Sloman leaving
and Emily starting.

02:08:03.360 --> 02:08:06.630
Obviously Jim continues to
work with us extremely closely,

02:08:06.630 --> 02:08:09.060
on elective recovery particularly.

02:08:09.060 --> 02:08:11.280
But incredibly grateful for him

02:08:11.280 --> 02:08:13.740
for stepping up and indeed for his ongoing

02:08:13.740 --> 02:08:15.210
support and hard work.

02:08:15.210 --> 02:08:16.934
- Well said, no, he's been brilliant.

02:08:16.934 --> 02:08:19.967
And then I was going to
say sort the best wishes

02:08:19.967 --> 02:08:20.800
for Christmas,

02:08:20.800 --> 02:08:22.710
although we're still
three weeks or so away.

02:08:22.710 --> 02:08:24.450
But also I do feel slightly awkward

02:08:24.450 --> 02:08:27.810
because winter is a real
challenge for the NHS.

02:08:27.810 --> 02:08:29.610
We're getting as ready as we can.

02:08:29.610 --> 02:08:31.590
We've had a long conversation about that.

02:08:31.590 --> 02:08:35.340
But still I think it's
best wishes for Christmas.

02:08:35.340 --> 02:08:38.490
And then just my huge thanks to the board,

02:08:38.490 --> 02:08:40.650
not often seen actually the amount of work

02:08:40.650 --> 02:08:42.693
the board does outside this boardroom.

02:08:44.075 --> 02:08:45.960
And that's not just in general interaction

02:08:45.960 --> 02:08:47.640
through the systems, in the trust

02:08:47.640 --> 02:08:50.550
and into the primary care
practises where we meet

02:08:50.550 --> 02:08:53.542
and also liaising with
other arms of government

02:08:53.542 --> 02:08:56.670
and other constituencies be
they think tanks and so on.

02:08:56.670 --> 02:08:57.960
We had, as we know dinner last night

02:08:57.960 --> 02:08:59.910
with and few the think tanks joined us,

02:09:00.982 --> 02:09:02.299
which was very good.

02:09:02.299 --> 02:09:03.609
But actually if I think about
the committees that we have,

02:09:03.609 --> 02:09:06.090
whether it's the audit and risk committee,

02:09:06.090 --> 02:09:09.830
whether it's the CFO advisory
group, whether it's people

02:09:09.830 --> 02:09:12.510
of remuneration committee,
whether it's the data digital

02:09:12.510 --> 02:09:16.450
and technology group
committee, workforce training

02:09:17.553 --> 02:09:18.840
and education committee,
and the quality committee.

02:09:18.840 --> 02:09:21.480
Now it sounds slightly
bureaucratic but it's not at all.

02:09:21.480 --> 02:09:24.270
They all oversee with non-executive

02:09:24.270 --> 02:09:26.906
director challenge important aspects

02:09:26.906 --> 02:09:29.582
of the work of NHS England.

02:09:29.582 --> 02:09:32.070
It's not seen, but it's a huge amount

02:09:32.070 --> 02:09:34.530
of work done by the board.

02:09:34.530 --> 02:09:35.760
And particularly in a year

02:09:35.760 --> 02:09:39.630
when actually we've combined
five entities and four boards.

02:09:39.630 --> 02:09:43.170
So consolidated the
agendas that are otherwise

02:09:43.170 --> 02:09:45.917
looked at by other separate boards

02:09:45.917 --> 02:09:47.250
and consolidated those
and have managed those.

02:09:47.250 --> 02:09:49.230
And this a unitary board

02:09:49.230 --> 02:09:51.457
or set of unitary board committees here.

02:09:51.457 --> 02:09:53.070
So my huge thanks to all of the non-execs

02:09:53.070 --> 02:09:55.839
actually for all the work that they do.

02:09:55.839 --> 02:09:56.970
And of course we've
established another committee,

02:09:56.970 --> 02:09:58.650
which is the genomics, the new board.

02:09:58.650 --> 02:10:01.170
The genomics board, which I
think is also really important

02:10:01.170 --> 02:10:04.140
given the transformational potential

02:10:04.140 --> 02:10:05.850
that genomics activity can have in the way

02:10:05.850 --> 02:10:08.100
we offer care.

02:10:08.100 --> 02:10:09.513
So thank you very much.

02:10:10.693 --> 02:10:12.600
At that point can I
call the board to close?

02:10:12.600 --> 02:10:13.433
Thank you.

